window.CORPUS = "Chapter 1: The Function Machine.\n\nBefore we begin.\n\nThis book was written to be listened to. That shapes it in a few ways. There are no equations read aloud, no tables, and no moments where I ask you to look at a figure. When an idea really wants a picture or a formula, I will describe it in words, and the picture itself lives on the companion website, where each chapter has a page of notes, the mathematics written out properly, links to the original papers, and a handful of interactive demonstrations you can play with when you are next at a screen.\n\nThe book has three parts. Part one is about mechanics: what a neural network is, how it learns, and why it has layers. Part two is about language: how we got from networks that recognise pictures of cats to the transformer, and from the transformer to machines that write. Part three is about the modern stack: how a raw language model becomes an assistant, how it is taught to reason and act, what we know about what goes on inside it, and what the people building these systems are arguing about right now.\n\nI am assuming you are comfortable with the ideas of a function, a derivative, a vector and a matrix, and that you have at some point fitted a curve to data. That is all you will need. If you have a background in physics or engineering, you will find that much of this field feels oddly familiar, and I will point out the family resemblances as we go.\n\nLet us start in the summer of 1958.\n\nThe machine that would be conscious.\n\nIn July of 1958, the United States Navy held a press conference in Washington to show off a new kind of computer program. It ran on an IBM 704, a machine the size of a room, and what it did was not, on its face, very impressive. It was shown punch cards, some marked on the left and some marked on the right. At first it guessed at random which was which. After about fifty cards it had taught itself to tell them apart.\n\nThe man behind it was Frank Rosenblatt, a twenty-nine-year-old research psychologist at the Cornell Aeronautical Laboratory in Buffalo. He called his creation the perceptron. And Rosenblatt was not shy about what it meant. The New York Times reported the next day that the Navy had revealed the embryo of a computer that it expected would one day walk, talk, see, write, reproduce itself and be conscious of its own existence.\n\nThat did not happen. Within about a decade, the perceptron was a discredited idea, its funding was drying up, and the phrase \"neural network\" was on its way to becoming something you avoided putting in a grant application. Rosenblatt died in a boating accident in 1971, on his forty-third birthday, before he could see any of it vindicated.\n\nAnd yet. The systems that today write software, pass medical licensing exams and hold conversations are direct descendants of that card-sorting program. Not metaphorical descendants. The basic unit Rosenblatt used is still the basic unit. The basic idea, that you do not program the machine but instead let it adjust itself in response to examples, is still the basic idea. What changed between 1958 and now is mostly a matter of how many units, how they are wired together, how they are adjusted, and how much data and computation is poured in. This book is the story of those changes.\n\nBut first we need to be clear about what the thing actually is.\n\nA function with knobs.\n\nHere is the least mystical description of a neural network I can give you. A neural network is a mathematical function with a very large number of adjustable parameters.\n\nIf you have a physics background, you have been doing a small version of this your whole career. Suppose you measure the extension of a spring under different loads. You plot the points, and you fit a straight line. A straight line is a family of functions with two adjustable parameters, the slope and the intercept. Fitting means finding the particular slope and intercept that make the line pass as close as possible to your data. Once you have them, you have a model: give it a new load, and it predicts an extension.\n\nNotice the structure of what you did. First, you chose a family of functions, in this case straight lines. Second, you defined what \"close\" means, probably the sum of the squared distances between the line and the points. Third, you had a procedure for finding the best parameters. For a straight line there is a neat formula. And at the end, all of the knowledge extracted from the data lives in two numbers.\n\nA neural network is exactly this, with three differences.\n\nThe first difference is scale. Instead of two parameters, there are millions, or billions, or by now trillions. The network known as GPT-3, released in 2020, had one hundred and seventy-five billion. Each one is just a number, like the slope of your line.\n\nThe second difference is the family of functions. In physics, you usually choose the form of the function from theory. You fit a straight line because Hooke's law says it should be a straight line. You fit an exponential because you believe in radioactive decay. The parameters mean something: a spring constant, a half-life. In a neural network, the family of functions is not chosen because theory says the answer has that form. It is chosen because it is enormously flexible, so flexible that it can approximate nearly any function you might care about, and because it turns out to be possible to fit. The individual parameters do not mean anything you could name. The millionth parameter is not the anything constant. This is a real philosophical shift, and it is worth sitting with. It is modelling without a theory of the thing being modelled.\n\nThe third difference is the fitting procedure. There is no neat formula. Instead there is a process of repeated small adjustment, which is called training, and which is the subject of the next chapter.\n\nThose parameters have a name you will hear constantly. They are called the weights. When someone says that a company has released the weights of a model, or that a lab is worried about its weights being stolen, this is what they mean: the list of numbers that the fitting procedure produced. A modern language model, stripped of everything else, is a file containing some hundreds of billions of numbers, plus a comparatively tiny piece of code, a few hundred lines, that says how to combine them with an input to produce an output. The code is public knowledge. The numbers are the crown jewels. They are what hundreds of millions of dollars' worth of electricity and chips were spent to find.\n\nSo that is the top-level picture. A function, with knobs, fitted to data. Now let us open the box and look at what kind of function.\n\nThe neuron.\n\nThe building block is called a neuron, by loose analogy to the cells in your brain, though I would encourage you not to take the analogy too seriously. An artificial neuron is a very simple little calculation.\n\nIt takes in some numbers; call them the inputs. Each input has its own weight. The neuron multiplies each input by its weight, and adds up the results. Then it adds one more number, called the bias, which does not depend on the inputs at all. Finally, it passes that total through a simple fixed function, called the activation function, and the result is the neuron's output. That is the whole thing. Multiply, add, squash.\n\nLet me make it concrete. Imagine a neuron whose job is to decide whether a small patch of an X-ray image contains a vertical edge. Say the patch is three pixels by three, so there are nine inputs, each one the brightness of a pixel. Give the three pixels in the left column weights of minus one. Give the three pixels in the right column weights of plus one. Give the middle column weights of zero. Now think about what the weighted sum does. If the patch is uniformly bright, or uniformly dark, the left and right columns cancel, and the sum is zero. If the patch is dark on the left and bright on the right, the sum is large and positive. The neuron responds strongly to exactly one kind of pattern, a dark-to-bright vertical edge, and the pattern it responds to is written directly in its weights.\n\nThat is the best first intuition for what weights are. A neuron's weights are a template. The weighted sum measures how well the input matches the template. In the language of linear algebra, it is a dot product between the input vector and the weight vector, and a dot product is a measure of alignment.\n\nThe bias sets the threshold: how good does the match have to be before the neuron cares? And the activation function turns the raw score into a response. In the earliest neurons, the activation function was a hard step: output one if the sum is above zero, and zero otherwise. The neuron fires or it does not. Later designs used smooth S-shaped curves, and the modern favourite is almost embarrassingly simple: if the sum is positive, pass it through unchanged, and if it is negative, output zero. That one is called the rectified linear unit, or ReLU, and it will make an important appearance in chapter four.\n\nWhy have an activation function at all? Why not just output the weighted sum? Hold that question. The answer turns out to be the key to why networks have layers, and we will get to it in chapter three.\n\nThere is a geometric way to see a neuron that I find helpful. Think of the inputs as coordinates of a point in space. With two inputs, it is a point on a plane. With nine inputs, it is a point in nine-dimensional space, which you cannot picture, but the mathematics does not care. The equation \"weighted sum plus bias equals zero\" defines a flat surface cutting through that space: a line in two dimensions, a plane in three, a hyperplane in general. On one side of it the neuron is on, and on the other side it is off. The weights set the orientation of that dividing surface, and the bias slides it back and forth. So a single neuron is a device for cutting space in two with a flat cut.\n\nLogic, learning and Rosenblatt's trick.\n\nThe idea of the artificial neuron dates to 1943, to a paper by an unlikely pair. Warren McCulloch was a neurophysiologist in his mid-forties. Walter Pitts was a homeless teenage runaway and self-taught logician of astonishing gifts. Together they showed that networks of simple threshold units, units that fire if enough of their inputs are active, could compute any logical function. You can build AND, OR and NOT from such units, and from those you can build anything. It was an existence proof that something brain-like could, in principle, compute.\n\nBut in the McCulloch and Pitts picture, somebody has to set the weights by hand. The network is designed, not taught. Rosenblatt's great contribution, fifteen years later, was a procedure for learning the weights from examples.\n\nHis procedure is simple enough to state in a breath. Show the perceptron an example. If it gets the answer right, do nothing. If it says \"no\" when the answer was \"yes\", then nudge each weight a little in the direction of the input, so that next time the weighted sum for that input will be higher. If it says \"yes\" when the answer was \"no\", nudge the weights the other way. Repeat, over and over, across all your examples.\n\nIn the geometric picture, each mistake tilts the dividing surface slightly toward getting that example right. And Rosenblatt and others proved a lovely theorem: if there exists any flat cut that separates the yes examples from the no examples, this procedure is guaranteed to find one in a finite number of steps.\n\nRosenblatt then built the thing in hardware. The Mark One Perceptron had a camera made from a grid of four hundred photocells, twenty by twenty. Its weights were physical: they were variable resistors, potentiometers, and when the machine learned, small electric motors turned the knobs. I love this detail, because it makes the abstraction literal. The weights really were knobs. Learning really was the turning of knobs. Everything since has been the same activity carried on by other means.\n\nThe limits of a single cut.\n\nSo what went wrong?\n\nThe trouble is in that phrase, \"if there exists a flat cut\". A single neuron can only divide the world with one flat surface. Many problems cannot be solved that way, and the standard example is almost insultingly small. It is called exclusive-or, or XOR. You have two inputs, each either zero or one. The answer should be \"yes\" if exactly one of the inputs is one, and \"no\" if they are both zero or both one.\n\nPicture the four possible inputs as the four corners of a square. The two \"yes\" cases sit at one pair of diagonally opposite corners. The two \"no\" cases sit at the other pair. Now try to draw a single straight line that puts both yes corners on one side and both no corners on the other. You cannot do it. It is geometrically impossible. And so a perceptron, no matter how long you train it, can never learn this trivially simple function.\n\nIn 1969, Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert of MIT published a book, titled simply Perceptrons, which worked out these limitations with mathematical rigour. Minsky and Rosenblatt had known each other since they were teenagers, having attended the same high school in the Bronx a year apart, and they had spent much of the sixties sparring at conferences. The book was elegant and correct, and it landed on a field that had made promises it could not keep.\n\nNow, the remedy for the XOR problem was known, even then. You use more than one layer. Have two neurons each make their own flat cut, and then have a third neuron look at the outputs of the first two and combine them. With two cuts you can carve out a band across the middle of the square, and the problem is solved. Minsky and Papert knew this perfectly well.\n\nThe difficulty was the learning rule. Rosenblatt's procedure works for a single layer because, when the output is wrong, you know exactly which weights to blame: the ones connected to the output. But when there is a layer of neurons in the middle, hidden between input and output, what should those neurons have done? Nobody tells you the right answer for a hidden neuron. If the final output is wrong, was it the fault of the last neuron, or of the middle ones that fed it bad information, and if so which, and by how much? This puzzle has a name: the credit assignment problem. In 1969 nobody had a practical solution to it, and Minsky and Papert wrote that they saw little reason to expect that one would be found.\n\nThe effect, combined with the general deflation of over-hyped artificial intelligence research at the time, was chilling. Funding moved elsewhere. Mainstream artificial intelligence spent the next two decades largely on a different programme, sometimes called symbolic AI, in which intelligence was to be achieved by writing down rules and logic explicitly, by hand. This period for neural networks is known as the first AI winter. A small band of researchers kept the idea alive, mostly at the margins, and we will meet several of them.\n\nWhere this leaves us.\n\nLet me gather up the vocabulary, because these words will carry through the rest of the book.\n\nA model is a function with adjustable parameters. The parameters of a neural network are its weights, plus the biases, though people often say \"weights\" to mean all of them. A neuron computes a weighted sum of its inputs, adds a bias, and applies an activation function. A network is many neurons wired together, with the outputs of some feeding the inputs of others. Training is the process of adjusting the weights so that the network's outputs match examples. Inference is what happens afterwards, when the weights are frozen and you simply run the function on new inputs. When you type a question into a chatbot, you are doing inference. Nothing in the model changes as a result.\n\nAnd we have two ideas that are worth holding on to. First, the weights of a neuron act as a template, and the neuron measures how well its input matches. Second, a single neuron can only make one flat cut through the space of its inputs, which is not enough, and the cure is layers.\n\nBut layers come with the credit assignment problem. If a network has a million weights buried in its middle, and its output is wrong, how do you work out which way to turn each of the million knobs?\n\nThe answer turned out to be something you learned in your first calculus course. It is the chain rule. Turning the chain rule into the engine that drives all of modern artificial intelligence is the subject of the next chapter.\n Chapter 2: Rolling Downhill.\n\nThe problem of the million knobs.\n\nImagine you are standing in front of a machine with a million knobs on it. You feed in a picture of a handwritten digit, a seven, say, and the machine lights up one of ten bulbs to tell you which digit it thinks it saw. At the moment the knobs are set at random, and the machine says \"three\". Your job is to turn the knobs until the machine gets it right, not just for this seven but for sixty thousand different handwritten digits.\n\nYou could try the obvious thing. Pick a knob, turn it a hair, and see whether the machine gets slightly better or slightly worse. Then put it back, and try the next knob. After a million such experiments you would know which way to nudge every knob, and you could nudge them all a little, and then begin again. This would work. It is also hopeless. Each round costs you a million runs of the machine, and you will need many thousands of rounds. For a modern network with a hundred billion weights, a single round would take longer than the age of the universe.\n\nWhat you want is some way to find out, in one go, how every knob affects the outcome. Not a million experiments, but one. That is what the algorithm called backpropagation gives you, and it is not an exaggeration to say that everything in modern artificial intelligence stands on it. Before we get to it, though, we need two preliminary ideas: a way to measure how wrong the machine is, and a strategy for using that measurement.\n\nHow wrong are we.\n\nThe first idea is the loss function. A loss function takes the network's output and the correct answer, and returns a single number that says how wrong the output was. Zero means perfect. Bigger means worse.\n\nYou already know one loss function: the sum of squared errors you use when fitting a line. If the network outputs a number and the right answer was a different number, take the difference and square it. For classification problems, like the digit recogniser, a different loss is used, which we will meet properly in chapter five, but the principle is identical. The network outputs a confidence for each possible answer, and the loss is small when it put high confidence on the right one and large when it did not.\n\nAverage the loss over all your training examples, and you get one number that summarises how well the network, with its current weights, fits the data. Change the weights, and that number changes. So the average loss is itself a function, a function of the weights. And this is the mental picture I want you to build. Imagine a landscape. The position on the map is the setting of the weights. The altitude at each position is the loss. High ground is bad; low ground is good. Training a network means finding your way to a low valley.\n\nOf course, the map does not have two dimensions. It has as many dimensions as there are weights, a million, or a hundred billion. But the picture of a landscape is still the right one, and physicists have a head start here, because it is precisely the picture of a potential energy surface. The system we are about to describe behaves like a ball rolling through thick honey on that surface, always sliding toward lower energy.\n\nFeeling the slope.\n\nThe second idea is the strategy, and it is called gradient descent.\n\nYou are on this landscape, and you are blindfolded. You cannot see where the valleys are. But you can feel the slope of the ground under your feet. So you do the natural thing: work out which direction is most steeply downhill, take a small step in that direction, and repeat.\n\nMathematically, the slope is the gradient: the list of partial derivatives of the loss with respect to every weight. Each entry answers the question, \"if I increased this one weight by a tiny amount, and left all the others alone, how much would the loss change?\" Collect all of those answers, and you have a vector that points in the direction of steepest ascent. Step the opposite way.\n\nHow big a step? That is set by a number called the learning rate, and it is the first example we have met of what is called a hyperparameter: a setting that is not learned by the network, but chosen by the human, usually by trial and error. Too small a learning rate, and training takes forever. Too large, and you leap clean across the valley and up the other side, and the loss, instead of falling, bounces around or explodes. A great deal of the practical craft of this field, more than its practitioners might like to admit, consists of getting numbers like the learning rate right.\n\nSo the whole of training comes down to one question. How do we compute the gradient? How do we get those hundred billion partial derivatives without doing a hundred billion experiments?\n\nThe chain rule, run backwards.\n\nHere is the setup. A network is a long chain of simple operations. The input goes into the first layer of neurons, which multiply by weights, add, and squash. Their outputs go into the second layer, which does the same. And so on, until the last layer produces the output, and the loss function turns that into a single number. It is a function of a function of a function, nested many levels deep.\n\nYou know how to differentiate a function of a function. That is the chain rule. If the loss depends on the output of the last layer, and that output depends on the layer before, then the sensitivity of the loss to the earlier layer is the product of the two sensitivities along the way.\n\nThe insight of backpropagation is about the order in which you do the bookkeeping. First, run the network forwards on an example, in the ordinary way, and remember every intermediate value: the output of every neuron. That is called the forward pass. Now start at the far end. Ask: how sensitive is the loss to each output of the final layer? That is easy; it is just the derivative of the loss function. Next, step back one layer. For each neuron in the second-to-last layer, ask how sensitive the loss is to its output. Well, that neuron influences the loss only by way of the final-layer neurons it feeds. You already know the sensitivity of the loss to each of those. So you just combine them, weighted by how strongly this neuron influences each one, and that strength is given by, of all things, the weights connecting them. Step back another layer and repeat. This is the backward pass.\n\nAt each stage you are reusing the work done for the layer after. A message of blame flows backwards through the network, from the output toward the input, and as it passes each weight, it tells that weight exactly how much it contributed to the error and in which direction. One forward pass, one backward pass, and you have the complete gradient: every partial derivative, for every weight, at a cost of roughly twice that of simply running the network. Not a hundred billion runs. Two.\n\nThat is the solution to the credit assignment problem from the last chapter. How do you know what a hidden neuron, buried in the middle, should have done? You do not need to know what it should have done. You only need to know which direction of change would have reduced the error, and the chain rule tells you.\n\nIf you work in imaging or inverse problems, you may have recognised this. It is the adjoint method. When you compute the gradient of a data-mismatch term with respect to an image by applying the adjoint of your forward operator, as in iterative tomographic reconstruction, where back-projection is the adjoint of projection, you are doing the same thing. Backpropagation is the adjoint method applied to a deep stack of non-linear operations. Applied mathematicians call the general technique reverse-mode automatic differentiation.\n\nThere is one technical consequence that matters for the history. For any of this to work, the network must be differentiable. Rosenblatt's neurons used a hard step for their activation function, either zero or one. The derivative of a step is zero everywhere, except at the jump, where it is undefined. No slope means no signal to follow. So the step had to be replaced with something smooth, at first an S-shaped curve called the sigmoid, which rises gently from zero to one. That small change, from a cliff to a ramp, was the price of admission.\n\nWho invented it.\n\nBackpropagation has a tangled paternity, as fundamental ideas often do. The underlying mathematics was developed in control theory around 1960, for computing optimal rocket trajectories. A Finnish master's student named Seppo Linnainmaa described reverse-mode automatic differentiation in 1970. An American, Paul Werbos, proposed in his 1974 doctoral thesis that it could be used to train neural networks, and found that almost no one was interested.\n\nThe version that changed the field appeared in the journal Nature in October 1986, under the title \"Learning representations by back-propagating errors\". It had three authors: David Rumelhart, a psychologist at the University of California in San Diego; Ronald Williams; and a young Englishman named Geoffrey Hinton.\n\nHinton is as close as this story has to a central character, so let me introduce him. He came from a remarkable family; his great-great-grandfather was George Boole, the inventor of the Boolean logic on which all digital computers run. He had drifted through physiology, philosophy and psychology at Cambridge, worked for a time as a carpenter, and finally took a doctorate in artificial intelligence at Edinburgh in the nineteen-seventies, at exactly the moment when neural networks were least fashionable. His supervisor tried, repeatedly, to talk him out of them. Hinton had a conviction, which he held with what can only be called stubbornness for the next thirty-five years, that the brain must work by adjusting the strengths of connections between neurons, and that the way to build an intelligent machine was to do the same.\n\nWhat the 1986 paper did was not so much to invent the algorithm as to demonstrate what it was good for. And the word to notice in the title is \"representations\". The authors showed that when you train a multi-layer network by backpropagation, the hidden neurons in the middle spontaneously come to represent meaningful features of the problem, features that nobody programmed in. In one example, a network given facts about two family trees, such as who is whose mother and who is whose uncle, developed internal units that encoded things like a person's generation, or which branch of the family they belonged to. The network had not been told that those concepts existed. It had invented them, because they were useful for getting the answers right.\n\nThat is the idea we will pick up in the next chapter. But first, some practicalities that will be with us for the rest of the book.\n\nStochastic gradient descent.\n\nThe landscape I described is defined by averaging the loss over all your training data. For a modern language model, the training data is a fair fraction of all the text ever written. Computing the true gradient would mean running the forward and backward pass over all of it before taking a single step.\n\nNobody does that. Instead, you grab a small random handful of examples, called a mini-batch, perhaps a few dozen or a few thousand. You compute the gradient just for those, take a step, and then grab another handful. The gradient from a handful is a noisy estimate of the true gradient, in the same way that a poll of a thousand people is a noisy estimate of an election. But it points roughly the right way, and you can take a thousand rough steps in the time it would take to compute one perfect one. This is called stochastic gradient descent, universally abbreviated to SGD. One complete pass through the training data is called an epoch.\n\nThe noise turns out to be not just tolerable but helpful. A physicist would say it acts like a temperature. It jostles the system, shaking it out of narrow crevices and letting it settle in broad, flat valleys. And there is good evidence that broad, flat valleys are precisely where you want to be: solutions there tend to work better on new data than solutions found in sharp, narrow pits.\n\nA few refinements are worth knowing by name. One is momentum: rather than following the current slope alone, let the weights accumulate velocity like a heavy ball, so that the motion smooths over small bumps and speeds up along consistent downhill directions. Another, from 2014, is an algorithm called Adam, which gives every individual weight its own automatically adjusted step size. Adam, or a close variant of it, is what trains almost every large model today. When you hear researchers mention the optimiser, this is the thing they mean: the specific recipe for turning gradients into weight updates.\n\nWhy does this work at all.\n\nThere is something that ought to bother you about all this. Gradient descent only ever goes downhill. So it should get stuck in the first hollow it comes to, the nearest local minimum, which might be far higher than the deepest valley. For a long time this was regarded as a decisive objection. The landscapes of neural networks are not simple bowls. They are fantastically rugged. Mathematicians knew how to guarantee good results for bowl-shaped problems, which they call convex, and neural networks were emphatically non-convex. For much of the nineteen-nineties and two-thousands, this was a respectable reason to dismiss the whole approach.\n\nIt turned out not to matter, and the reason is a nice piece of high-dimensional intuition. To be stuck in a true local minimum, the ground must curve upward in every direction you could possibly move. In two dimensions that is common; any dimple will do. But in a billion dimensions, it requires that all billion independent directions curve upward at once. If each direction is even roughly a coin toss, that essentially never happens. What you find instead are saddle points, places that curve up in some directions and down in others, and from a saddle, there is always a way out. The few true minima that do exist tend to be about as good as one another. In very high dimensions, the landscape is less like a mountain range full of traps and more like a vast, gently connected system of valleys.\n\nNobody proved this in advance. People tried it, and it worked, and the theoretical understanding came afterwards and is still incomplete. That is a pattern you will see repeatedly in this field. It is an empirical science, closer in spirit to experimental physics than to mathematics, and the theory is usually running to catch up with the experiments.\n\nThe elephant in the room.\n\nThere is a second thing that ought to bother you, and it will bother you more if you are a physicist.\n\nJohn von Neumann is supposed to have said that with four parameters he could fit an elephant, and with five he could make it wiggle its trunk. The point is one that every experimentalist has had drilled into them: a model with too many free parameters will fit anything, including the noise in your data, and will therefore predict nothing. This is called overfitting. A network with a billion parameters, trained on a million examples, has every opportunity to simply memorise the examples. Its loss on the training data would go to zero, and it would be useless on anything new.\n\nThe basic defence is methodological, and it is so central to the culture of machine learning that it is worth stating plainly. You never judge a model by its performance on the data it was trained on. You hold some data back, called the test set, which the model never sees during training, and you measure performance on that. What you care about is generalisation: how well the model does on examples it has not seen. Nearly every claim you will ever hear about a model's ability is, underneath, a claim about performance on some held-out test set, known as a benchmark, and a surprising number of arguments in the field come down to whether a particular benchmark was really held out.\n\nBut the deeper puzzle remains. By all the classical statistical wisdom, these gigantic models should overfit catastrophically. They do not. They generalise remarkably well, and they generalise better as they get bigger. Why that should be is one of the genuinely deep questions about this technology, and I will come back to it in chapter four, once we have seen the networks that forced the question.\n\nThe loop.\n\nLet me put the pieces together, because this loop is the heartbeat of everything that follows.\n\nYou start with a network whose weights are random numbers. You take a batch of examples. You run them through the network: the forward pass. You compare the outputs with the right answers and compute the loss. You run backpropagation, the backward pass, to get the gradient: the direction in which each weight should move to reduce the loss. You move every weight a small step in that direction. Then you take another batch, and do it again. And again. Millions of times.\n\nThat is training. It is not a metaphor for something more sophisticated. The most capable artificial intelligence systems in the world were made by this loop, running for months on tens of thousands of specialised chips. Nobody tells the network what features to look for or what concepts to form. There is only a number that measures error, and a procedure that relentlessly makes that number smaller. Everything the network knows, it knows because knowing it helped push the loss downhill.\n\nWhich raises the question of what, exactly, the hidden layers in the middle come to know. Why do we need them, why should there be many, and what are they doing? That is where we go next.\n Chapter 3: Why Depth.\n\nA question left hanging.\n\nIn chapter one I left a question hanging. A neuron computes a weighted sum of its inputs and then passes it through an activation function, a little non-linear squashing step. Why bother with the squashing? Why not just pass along the weighted sum?\n\nThe answer is that without it, layers would be pointless. Suppose every neuron simply output its weighted sum. A layer of such neurons takes a vector of inputs and produces a vector of outputs, each a weighted sum of the inputs. That is a linear transformation; in other words, it is multiplication by a matrix. Now stack a second layer on top. That is another matrix multiplication. But a matrix times a matrix is just another matrix. Two linear layers are exactly equivalent to one linear layer with different weights, and so are ten, and so are a thousand. You can stack linear operations as high as you like, and you never get anything richer than a single one. You are still making flat cuts.\n\nThe non-linearity is what breaks this collapse. Put a bend between each pair of matrix multiplications, and the stack can no longer be simplified. Each layer can now do something that the layers before it could not. So the recipe for a neural network is an alternation: linear, bend, linear, bend. A matrix multiplication to mix the information, a simple non-linear function applied to each number separately, and repeat. Almost every network in this book, including the giant language models, is a variation on that alternation.\n\nA network built in the plainest way, with each neuron in one layer connected to every neuron in the next, is called a multi-layer perceptron, or MLP. The layers between input and output are the hidden layers. The number of layers is the depth of the network, and the number of neurons in a layer is its width. \"Deep learning\" simply means learning with networks of many layers. The name is a description of the geometry, not a claim about profundity.\n\nLayers as changes of coordinates.\n\nSo what do the layers do? The best way I know to think about it is this: each layer re-describes its input in a new coordinate system, one in which the problem is a little easier.\n\nGo back to the XOR problem. Four points at the corners of a square, with the \"yes\" answers on one diagonal and the \"no\" answers on the other, and no single straight line that can separate them. A two-layer network solves it like this. The hidden layer has two neurons. One of them learns to fire when at least one input is on. The other learns to fire only when both inputs are on. Now forget the original inputs, and describe each of the four cases by what those two hidden neurons are doing. In that new description, in those new coordinates, the four points have moved. The \"yes\" cases are the ones where the first hidden neuron is on and the second is off. And that condition can be checked with a single flat cut. The output neuron makes that cut, and the problem is solved.\n\nThe hidden layer did not solve the problem. It transformed the problem into one that a simple linear cut could solve. This is the general principle. The last layer of nearly every network is doing something very simple, essentially drawing flat surfaces. All the layers before it are there to bend, stretch and fold the space of inputs until the things you want to tell apart are separable by flat surfaces.\n\nPicture two categories of data tangled together like two crumpled sheets of paper, one red and one blue, wadded up in a ball. No flat cut will separate the red from the blue. Each layer of the network is one move in un-crumpling the ball: a stretch here, a fold there. After enough moves, the red sheet is on one side and the blue on the other, and a single cut divides them. On the companion site, there is a demo that lets you watch a small network do precisely this to points on a plane, and I would encourage you to try it, because it makes the idea visceral in a way that words cannot.\n\nThe vector of neuron outputs at a given layer has a name, which you will hear constantly. It is called a representation: the network's internal description of the input at that stage of processing. People also call these vectors activations, or sometimes embeddings. The claim of deep learning, the thing asserted in the title of that 1986 paper, is that backpropagation discovers useful representations by itself. You specify only the input and the desired output. The intermediate descriptions, the coordinates in which the problem becomes easy, are found by the training process.\n\nTo appreciate how radical that was, you have to know how things were done before. If you wanted a computer to recognise objects in images in the year 2005, you did not feed it pixels. You first computed features, designed by clever humans: measures of edge orientations, of texture, of corner-ness, with names like SIFT and HOG. You fed those hand-crafted features to a comparatively simple learning algorithm. The intelligence was in the feature design, and a doctoral thesis might be devoted to a better feature. This was called feature engineering, and it was most of the work. Deep learning proposed to make it obsolete, by learning the features and the classifier together, end to end, from raw data. For those of you in medical imaging, this is the transition from hand-built radiomic features and tuned filter pipelines to networks that take the raw image in at one end.\n\nIf one layer is enough, why use many.\n\nHere is a fact that seems, at first, to undercut the whole enterprise. In 1989, a mathematician named George Cybenko proved what is now called the universal approximation theorem. It says that a network with just a single hidden layer can approximate any reasonable function as closely as you like, provided the hidden layer is wide enough.\n\nSo depth is not strictly needed. One hidden layer can, in principle, do everything. Why, then, do modern networks have dozens or hundreds of layers?\n\nThe catch is in the words \"wide enough\". The theorem tells you that a solution exists. It does not tell you how many neurons it needs, and for many functions of interest, the answer is an astronomically large number. A single hidden layer approximates a function the way a lookup table does, by devoting a few neurons to each little region of the input space. That works, but the number of regions grows exponentially with the number of input dimensions.\n\nDepth offers an escape, because depth allows reuse. Think about how you would write a computer program. You could, in principle, write any program as one gigantic flat table listing the right output for every possible input. Nobody does that. You write small functions, and then functions that call those functions, and then functions that call those. Each level builds on the one below, and a small amount of code yields an enormous range of behaviour. A deep network can do the same thing. A feature computed in layer two can be used by a hundred different features in layer three, each of which can be used by a hundred in layer four. The number of distinct patterns the network can express grows exponentially with depth, while the number of weights grows only in proportion. There are functions that a deep network can represent with thousands of neurons, and that a shallow network would need more neurons than there are atoms in the universe to match.\n\nNow, that only helps if the functions we care about have that kind of structure: wholes made from parts, made from smaller parts. And here is the remarkable thing. The world does seem to be like that. An image of a face is made of eyes and a nose and a mouth. An eye is made of curves and patches of texture. A curve is made of short edges. An edge is a particular arrangement of a few pixels. Language is the same: letters, words, phrases, sentences, arguments. Physical law is local and compositional, and the structure of the things in the world reflects that. Deep networks work, in this view, because their architecture mirrors the hierarchical structure of the data. A physicist might be reminded of the renormalisation group, in which you describe a system at successively coarser scales, each built from the one below. The analogy is loose, but people have taken it seriously.\n\nAnd this is not just a story. When researchers trained deep networks on images and then went looking at what the individual neurons responded to, they found exactly this hierarchy. Neurons in the first layer respond to edges at particular orientations and to blobs of colour. In the next layers, to corners, and textures, and simple patterns. Further up, to parts of objects: eyes, wheels, fur, text. And near the top, to whole objects. Nobody designed that. It emerged from gradient descent.\n\nSeeing like a cat.\n\nThat hierarchy had been found once before, in a different kind of network. In 1959, two neurophysiologists at Johns Hopkins, David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel, were recording from single neurons in the visual cortex of an anaesthetised cat, while projecting patterns onto a screen in front of it. They found cells that responded not to spots of light, but to edges: a bar of light at one particular angle, in one particular place. Other cells, a stage further along, responded to an edge at a given angle anywhere within a larger region. They called these simple cells and complex cells, and they proposed that the visual system is built as a hierarchy, with each stage assembling more elaborate features from the simpler ones before it. The work won them a Nobel Prize.\n\nThe person who turned that insight into a trainable machine was a Frenchman named Yann LeCun. LeCun had come across neural networks as an engineering student in Paris in the early nineteen-eighties, when hardly anyone in France was working on them, and had independently worked out a version of backpropagation. He spent a year as a postdoctoral researcher with Geoffrey Hinton in Toronto, and in 1988 he joined Bell Labs in New Jersey, at that time perhaps the greatest industrial research laboratory in the world. There he set out to get a network to read handwritten digits; specifically, the zip codes on envelopes, from data provided by the United States Postal Service.\n\nHis design is called a convolutional neural network, and if you work in imaging, you already understand most of it.\n\nThink about the edge-detecting neuron from chapter one: nine weights, arranged three by three, looking at one small patch of an image. A plain, fully connected network would need a separate such neuron, with its own separately learned weights, for every position in the image. That is wasteful, and worse, it ignores something we know perfectly well about images: an edge is an edge, wherever it happens to be. So LeCun's network uses the same nine weights at every position. You slide the little template across the whole image, and at each location record how well the image matches. What you get is a new image, a map showing where that feature occurs.\n\nYou will recognise this operation. It is a convolution of the image with a small kernel. It is the same thing you do when you apply a Sobel filter to find edges, or a Gaussian to smooth. The one difference, and it is the whole point, is that the numbers in the kernel are not designed by an engineer. They are weights, learned by backpropagation like any others. A convolutional layer learns a bank of such filters, a few dozen or a few hundred, each producing its own feature map. The next layer then applies learned filters to those maps, finding patterns among the patterns. In between, the maps are typically shrunk, by a step called pooling, so that higher layers see larger regions of the image at coarser resolution. Edges, then combinations of edges, then combinations of those: Hubel and Wiesel's hierarchy, made out of learned convolutions.\n\nThis design builds two assumptions about images into the wiring of the network. One is locality: nearby pixels are related, and features are built from small neighbourhoods. The other is translation symmetry: a feature is the same thing wherever it appears. Assumptions of this kind have a name which is very much worth knowing. They are called inductive biases. An inductive bias is knowledge about the problem that is built into the architecture, as opposed to being learned from the data. A convolutional network does not need to learn that a cat in the top left is the same as a cat in the bottom right, because it is incapable of believing otherwise. And because the same few weights are reused everywhere, it needs vastly fewer parameters and far less data than a fully connected network would.\n\nRemember the idea of inductive bias. A lot of the history that follows can be read as an argument about how much of it you want. More built-in structure means learning faster from less data. But it also means the network can only learn what its builders thought to permit. We will see that argument come to a head in chapter nine.\n\nLeCun's network worked. By the late nineteen-nineties, a system based on it was reading the handwritten amounts on a substantial fraction of all the cheques deposited in the United States. It was a real, deployed, commercially valuable neural network. And the wider field of artificial intelligence, by and large, shrugged.\n\nThe second winter.\n\nWhy the shrug? Because, outside of a few niches like digit recognition, neural networks in the nineteen-nineties did not work very well, and there was a good reason.\n\nConvolutional networks for digits were only a few layers deep. When people tried to train properly deep networks, with ten layers or twenty, training failed. The network just would not learn. The cause was identified in 1991 by a German student named Sepp Hochreiter, in a diploma thesis that was written in German and went largely unread for years. It is called the vanishing gradient problem.\n\nRecall how backpropagation works. The error signal is passed backwards through the network, layer by layer, and at each layer it gets multiplied by a factor that depends on the weights and on the slope of the activation function. With the S-shaped sigmoid function that everybody used, that slope is never more than a quarter, and is usually much less. So at each layer, on its way back, the signal shrinks. Go back through ten layers, and you are multiplying ten small numbers together. The gradient decays exponentially with depth. By the time it reaches the early layers, it is indistinguishable from zero, and those layers, receiving no signal about which way to move, do not learn. Occasionally the factors are larger than one, and then the opposite happens: the gradient grows exponentially and training blows up.\n\nSo the very thing that makes deep networks powerful, the long chain of composition, made them untrainable. Depth was a fine theory which nobody could use.\n\nMeanwhile, rival methods were flourishing. The most successful was the support vector machine, developed by Vladimir Vapnik, who, as it happens, worked down the corridor from LeCun at Bell Labs. Support vector machines came with something neural networks lacked: mathematical guarantees. The optimisation problem was convex, a single bowl, with one minimum that you were certain to find. There was a rigorous theory of how well the result would generalise. They performed as well as neural networks or better on the benchmarks of the day. To a serious-minded researcher around the year 2000, neural networks looked like alchemy: a bag of unprincipled tricks for a non-convex problem, with too many knobs and no theory. Papers that used them were routinely rejected from the main conferences, sometimes without much of a reading. This was the second winter.\n\nThree people, above all, refused to give up. Geoffrey Hinton, by then at the University of Toronto. Yann LeCun, who left industry for New York University. And Yoshua Bengio, at the University of Montreal, who had overlapped with LeCun at Bell Labs. In 2004, Hinton persuaded a Canadian research foundation, known as CIFAR, to fund a small programme that would bring together the scattered believers for regular workshops. It was modest money, but it kept a community alive. They began, quite deliberately, to use a new name for their work, one that carried none of the baggage of the old. They called it deep learning.\n\nIn 2006, Hinton published a method for training deep networks one layer at a time, which sidestepped the vanishing gradient problem and showed that depth really did pay off. The particular technique was soon superseded and is no longer used. But it rekindled interest, and it sent a generation of graduate students to Toronto, Montreal and New York. In 2018, Hinton, LeCun and Bengio would share the Turing Award, computing's highest honour, for work that for most of their careers had been regarded as a dead end.\n\nWhat was missing.\n\nLooking back from here, what is striking about this period is how much was already in place. By 1990, the field had the neuron. It had backpropagation and gradient descent. It had multi-layer networks, and the understanding that hidden layers learn representations. It had convolutional networks. Nearly every conceptual ingredient of the revolution that was coming already existed, a quarter of a century before it arrived.\n\nWhat was missing was not ideas. It was two rather more mundane things: data, and computing power. The networks of the nineties were trained on thousands of examples, on computers a million times slower than the ones we have now. Their creators believed, on not much evidence, that the same methods would do extraordinary things if only they could be scaled up. Most of their colleagues believed that if a method does not work at small scale, there is no reason to think it will work at large scale, which is, to be fair, usually true.\n\nIn 2012, the believers got their experiment. The result was so decisive that it ended the argument more or less overnight, and it is the subject of the next chapter.\n Chapter 4: The ImageNet Moment.\n\nTwo missing ingredients.\n\nAt the end of the last chapter, the believers in neural networks had their ideas in place, and lacked two things: data, and computing power. This chapter is about how each arrived, from directions nobody in the field had planned, and what happened when they met.\n\nStart with the data. In 2006, a young professor named Fei-Fei Li had an idea that most of her colleagues thought was a poor use of a promising career. Computer vision research at the time was focused on algorithms: cleverer features, better classifiers. The datasets used to test them were small, a few thousand images in a few dozen categories. Li suspected the field had its priorities backwards. A child learns to see by looking at an enormous variety of things, for years. Perhaps what the algorithms lacked was not cleverness but experience.\n\nSo she set out to build a dataset on the scale of the visual world: every category of object for which English has a noun, with hundreds or thousands of example photographs of each. The plan called for labelling millions of images by hand. Her first estimate, using undergraduates, was that it would take around ninety years. What rescued the project was a then-new service from Amazon called Mechanical Turk, which let her farm out tiny labelling tasks to tens of thousands of people around the world, for pennies each. By 2009, the dataset, which she called ImageNet, held millions of labelled images in thousands of categories. She presented it at the leading vision conference that year, where it was relegated to a poster session. Few people saw the point.\n\nTo attract attention, Li and her colleagues started an annual competition. Teams were given about 1.2 million training images drawn from a thousand categories, among them a hundred and twenty different breeds of dog, and their systems were then scored on images they had not seen. The standard measure was the top-five error rate: the system is allowed five guesses, and it is marked wrong if none of them is right. In 2010 the winning entry got about twenty-eight per cent wrong. In 2011, about twenty-six. Progress was real, but incremental, and all the top entries were built on hand-engineered features.\n\nChips made for games.\n\nThe second ingredient came from video games.\n\nDrawing a three-dimensional scene on a screen, sixty times a second, means doing the same simple arithmetic on millions of pixels and triangles at once. Through the nineteen-nineties and two-thousands, a competitive industry grew up building chips specialised for exactly that: graphics processing units, or GPUs. Where an ordinary processor has a handful of powerful cores that do complicated things one after another, a GPU has thousands of simple ones that all do the same thing at the same time.\n\nNow think about what a neural network needs. The forward pass is a matrix multiplication followed by a simple function applied to every element. The backward pass is the same. Matrix multiplication is the perfect parallel job: every entry of the result can be computed independently of every other. The arithmetic of neural networks and the arithmetic of video games turn out to be nearly the same arithmetic.\n\nThe company that saw furthest was Nvidia. In 2007, at the insistence of its chief executive, Jensen Huang, Nvidia released a software system called CUDA, which let programmers use its graphics cards for general scientific computing. It was a costly bet on a market that barely existed, and for years investors grumbled about it. By around 2009, a few neural network researchers had noticed that a pair of gaming cards costing a thousand dollars could train their networks ten to fifty times faster than a conventional processor. A factor of fifty turns an experiment that would take a year into one that takes a week. It is the difference between a thing being impossible in practice and being a student project.\n\nThe bedroom in Toronto.\n\nWhich brings us to two graduate students in Geoffrey Hinton's lab in Toronto.\n\nAlex Krizhevsky was quiet, and an exceptionally gifted programmer, with a knack for coaxing performance out of GPUs. Ilya Sutskever was intense and visionary, and had an unshakeable belief that neural networks, made large enough and given enough data, would simply work. Both had been born in the Soviet Union and come to Canada as children. It was Sutskever who looked at ImageNet and saw that it was the experiment the field had been waiting for: a dataset big enough to feed a truly large network. He talked Krizhevsky into taking it on.\n\nKrizhevsky wrote the code for a convolutional network, far larger than anything LeCun had built, and trained it on two Nvidia gaming cards, which sat in a computer in his bedroom at his parents' house. Training took the better part of a week for each attempt. The network had eight layers, about six hundred and fifty thousand neurons, and sixty million weights.\n\nIn the autumn of 2012, the results of that year's ImageNet competition were announced. The second-best entry, a refined version of the conventional approach, had a top-five error rate of about twenty-six per cent, about the same as the previous year's winner. The Toronto network, which became known as AlexNet, scored fifteen per cent.\n\nIn a field where a one-point improvement was a good year, that was not an incremental advance. It was a discontinuity. An approach that most of the vision community had written off had not merely won; it had made everything else look obsolete. People who were in the room when the results were presented describe a mixture of excitement and something close to dismay. Within two years, essentially every entry in the competition was a deep convolutional network, and the feature engineers had either converted or retired from the field. By 2015 the winning error rate was under four per cent, better than the roughly five per cent that a well-trained human manages on the same test.\n\nWhat made it work.\n\nAlexNet was, conceptually, LeCun's network from 1989, made bigger. But it did include a few new tricks that made bigness possible, and they are worth understanding, because they show you the kind of thing that counts as progress in this field.\n\nThe most important was the activation function. Recall the vanishing gradient problem: the sigmoid function's slope is small, so that the error signal shrinks as it travels back through each layer. AlexNet used the rectified linear unit instead, the ReLU, which had been championed a couple of years earlier by Hinton's group and by Bengio's. The ReLU outputs zero if its input is negative, and passes the input through unchanged if it is positive. It looks too simple to be a serious non-linearity: it is just a ramp with a kink at zero. But on the positive side its slope is exactly one. Gradients pass back through it without shrinking at all. Networks using it trained several times faster, and could be made deeper. That a change this trivial could matter this much tells you something about how unexplored the territory was.\n\nThe second trick was aimed at overfitting, and it is called dropout. During training, each time an example is processed, you randomly switch off half of the neurons, a different random half each time. This sounds like sabotage. But it prevents the network from relying too heavily on any one neuron, or on fragile conspiracies among particular neurons, and forces it to spread knowledge redundantly. Hinton has said the idea came to him at his bank, where he noticed that the tellers kept being rotated, and realised that this would make it hard for any group of them to collude to defraud the bank.\n\nThe third was simply to manufacture more data. Take each training image and make shifted copies, mirrored copies, copies with slightly altered colours. A mirrored cat is still a cat, and the network learns from all of them. This is called data augmentation.\n\nAnd the fourth was the GPU, without which none of the rest would have mattered.\n\nThe auction.\n\nThe aftermath is one of the stranger episodes in the history of technology. Hinton, Krizhevsky and Sutskever formed a company. It had no products and no plans for any; its assets were the three of them. In December 2012, during the field's main annual conference, held that year at a casino hotel on the shore of Lake Tahoe, Hinton auctioned the company off by email from his hotel room. Four bidders took part: Google, Microsoft, the Chinese search company Baidu, and a small London start-up called DeepMind. The bidding went up in million-dollar increments. When it reached forty-four million dollars, Hinton stopped the auction and sold to Google, which is what he had wanted to do all along.\n\nThat auction was the starting gun for a talent war. Facebook hired Yann LeCun to found an artificial intelligence laboratory. Google bought DeepMind, for a reported sum of more than half a billion dollars. Graduate students who knew how to train neural networks, of whom there were at that point perhaps a few hundred in the world, found themselves being offered the salaries of professional athletes. Research that had lived in a handful of university labs moved, within about three years, into the largest and richest corporations on earth. That shift, in who does the research and who pays for it, has shaped everything since.\n\nGoing deeper.\n\nAfter 2012, a kind of gold rush set in to make networks deeper, and it soon ran into a wall. ReLU had eased the vanishing gradient problem, but it had not removed it. Beyond about twenty layers, something perverse happened: deeper networks did worse than shallower ones, and not just on test data, but on their own training data. That could not be overfitting. It was a failure of optimisation. The deeper network could, in principle, have copied the shallower one and left its extra layers doing nothing; but gradient descent could not find even that solution.\n\nA handful of innovations between 2014 and 2016 knocked the wall down. Each of them is now part of the standard kit, and all of them are inside every large language model, so let me go through them.\n\nThe first was better optimisers; Adam, which I mentioned in chapter two, dates from 2014. The second was careful initialisation: choosing the scale of the initial random weights so that signals neither grow nor shrink, on average, as they pass through each layer. This is an essentially physical argument about keeping the variance of a signal constant through a chain of amplifiers.\n\nThe third was normalisation. If you worry that the numbers flowing through the network will drift to be far too large or too small, then just force them not to: at each layer, rescale the activations to have a mean of zero and a standard deviation of one. The first version of this, called batch normalisation, came out of Google in 2015, and it made deep networks dramatically easier to train. A later variant, layer normalisation, is the one used in transformers.\n\nThe fourth, and the one I most want you to remember, was the residual connection. It came in late 2015 from a team at Microsoft's research laboratory in Beijing, led by Kaiming He. The idea is this. Ordinarily, each layer takes its input and transforms it into something new, which replaces the input. In a residual network, each layer instead computes a correction, which is then added to its input. The output is the input, plus a learned adjustment.\n\nWhy should that help? Think about the path of the gradient on its way back. In an ordinary network, it has to pass through every layer's transformation, getting distorted at each one. With residual connections, there is an express lane: the addition passes the gradient straight back, unchanged, around each layer. Signals can travel from the output all the way to the earliest layers without ever being squeezed. And think about what a layer has to do if it has nothing useful to contribute. In an ordinary network it would have to learn to reproduce its input exactly, which is surprisingly hard. In a residual network it just outputs zero.\n\nIf you have ever integrated a differential equation numerically, a residual network will look familiar. Each layer says: the new state is the old state plus a small increment that depends on the old state. That is one step of Euler's method. A residual network is, in a sense, a system evolving through time, with depth playing the role of time, and that analogy has been made mathematically precise.\n\nWith residual connections, He's team trained a network a hundred and fifty-two layers deep, and won the 2015 ImageNet competition. Soon afterwards people trained networks of a thousand layers, simply to show that it could be done. The depth barrier was gone. Their paper went on to become one of the most cited scientific papers of the century, in any field. When we open up the transformer in chapter seven, you will find residual connections running through it like a spine.\n\nWhat are architects actually designing.\n\nThis is a good moment to step back and answer a question you may have been forming. When researchers design a network architecture, what is it that they are designing? What are the choices, and what are they trying to achieve? I think there are four things.\n\nThe first is inductive bias: building the right assumptions into the wiring. Convolution assumes that images are local and translation-symmetric. We will see other architectures that build in assumptions about sequences. The art lies in choosing assumptions that are true of your data, and general enough not to get in the way.\n\nThe second is trainability. An architecture is not merely a family of functions; it is a family of functions that gradient descent must be able to search. Many of the important innovations, the ReLU, normalisation, residual connections, do nothing to change what the network can represent in principle. They change how easily gradients flow through it. A large part of architecture design is plumbing for gradients.\n\nThe third is computational efficiency: getting the most learning for each unit of computation, on the hardware that actually exists. An idea that cannot be expressed as big matrix multiplications on a GPU is, in practice, an idea that will lose to one that can. Hardware and architectures have evolved together.\n\nAnd the fourth is not architecture at all, but it matters at least as much: the choice of what data to train on and what loss to minimise. What question, exactly, are you asking the network to get good at? In part two we will see that the right choice of question turned out to be the most powerful lever of all.\n\nI should also be honest about the method by which these designs are found. There is very little theory that can tell you ahead of time whether an architecture will work. People have intuitions; they try things; they keep what helps. The field has a wry name for this, a play on gradient descent: graduate student descent.\n\nThe puzzle of generalisation.\n\nBefore we leave part one, I owe you a return to the elephant. Why do these enormous networks not overfit?\n\nThe puzzle was put sharply in a 2016 paper with the provocative title \"Understanding deep learning requires rethinking generalization\". The authors took a standard image dataset and replaced all the labels with random ones, so that there was nothing to learn; the picture of a cat might be labelled \"aeroplane\". They then trained a standard network on this garbage. The network achieved a perfect score on the training data. It simply memorised a million arbitrary labels. So these networks certainly have the capacity to memorise anything at all. And yet, trained on real labels, the very same network does not just memorise; it finds patterns that hold on new images. Classical statistical theory says that a model which is capable of fitting random noise cannot be trusted to generalise. Here was one that did.\n\nA related finding is called double descent. The textbook picture says that as you make a model bigger, its error on new data first falls, as it becomes able to capture the real pattern, and then rises, as it starts to fit the noise. And that is what you see, up to a point. But keep going, past the point where the model is just big enough to fit the training data perfectly, and the test error turns round and starts to fall again, often to below its previous best. The textbook picture is only the left-hand half of the true curve.\n\nThe best current understanding runs something like this. When a model has far more parameters than it needs, there is not one setting of the weights that fits the training data, but a vast continuum of them. Some are wild, wiggly functions that generalise terribly. Others are smooth and simple. So the question becomes which one training picks. And it appears that gradient descent, starting from small random weights, has a built-in preference for the simple ones. It is not that the network could not overfit. It is that the training procedure quietly favours solutions that do not. This preference is called implicit regularisation, and it is not written anywhere in the code. It is an emergent property of the dynamics. A physicist might say that the simple solutions occupy overwhelmingly more of the volume of weight space, so that a noisy search finds them for much the same reason that a gas fills its container.\n\nThis is an active area of research, and it is not a closed question. But the practical lesson was absorbed quickly, and it overturned the instincts of a generation of statisticians. Bigger models are not more dangerous. Bigger models are better. Hold on to that thought, because by the time we reach chapter nine it will have been turned into an industrial strategy.\n\nThe end of the beginning.\n\nIn the four years after AlexNet, deep learning swept through one field after another. Speech recognition went first; the error rates of the voice recognition on your phone fell by a quarter almost overnight. Then image recognition, then medical image analysis, then machine translation. In March 2016, a system built by DeepMind, called AlphaGo, defeated Lee Sedol, one of the strongest players in the world, at the game of Go, a feat that most experts had believed to be at least a decade away.\n\nBut one domain was proving stubborn, and it was the one that mattered most. Language. Networks could label a photograph and transcribe a spoken sentence, but they could not really read, or write, or hold up their end of a conversation. The architectures that conquered vision were not suited to it. Language is a sequence; its meaning depends on order, and on connections between words that may be far apart. To handle it, the field needed a different way of turning words into numbers, and a different kind of network.\n\nThat is the story of part two.\n Chapter 5: Words into Vectors.\n\nThe guessing game.\n\nIn about 1950, Claude Shannon played a game with his wife, Betty. He would pick a passage from a book, and she would try to guess it, one letter at a time. For each letter she guessed until she got it right, and he wrote down how many guesses it had taken. Then on to the next letter.\n\nShannon was, at that point, two years on from publishing the paper that founded information theory, and he was using the game to measure something: the amount of information in written English. What he found was that Betty needed very few guesses. Most of the time she got the letter on her first try. Given the text so far, the next letter of English is extremely predictable. Shannon calculated that each letter carries, on average, only about one bit of real information, when in principle a choice among twenty-six letters and a space could carry nearly five. English is about three-quarters redundant.\n\nThink about what Betty Shannon was using to play that game. She was using her knowledge of spelling, obviously. And of grammar. But she was also using her knowledge of what the passage was about, and of what kinds of things people say, and of how the world works. To predict that the next word after \"the surgeon picked up the\" is more likely to be \"scalpel\" than \"trombone\", you have to know something about surgery. Predicting text well requires understanding it, and the better your understanding, the better your predictions.\n\nThat observation is the foundation of everything in part two of this book. The entire technology of large language models is the playing of Shannon's guessing game by a neural network: given the text so far, predict what comes next. It sounds like a party trick. It turned out to be a way of pouring human knowledge into a machine.\n\nWhat is a language model.\n\nLet me define terms. A language model is a system that, given a sequence of words, assigns a probability to every possible next word. After \"the cat sat on the\", it might put thirty per cent on \"mat\", ten per cent on \"floor\", eight per cent on \"sofa\", and a tiny fraction of a per cent on \"parliament\".\n\nThere is a simple fact from probability theory that makes this more powerful than it sounds. The probability of a whole sentence is the probability of its first word, times the probability of the second word given the first, times the probability of the third given the first two, and so on. So a model that can predict the next word implicitly defines a probability for every possible text of any length. Nothing has been lost by reducing the problem to one word at a time.\n\nAnd once you have such a model, you can make it write. Give it a starting text. Ask for the probabilities of the next word. Pick a word according to those probabilities, as though rolling a weighted die. Add it to the end of the text. Then ask again. Each new word is chosen in light of everything written so far, including the words the model has just produced itself. This loop has a name, autoregressive generation, and it is exactly what is happening when you watch a chatbot's answer appear on your screen a word at a time. That is not a visual effect. The model really is producing one piece at a time, and each piece is fed back in to produce the next.\n\nCounting, and its limits.\n\nShannon himself built the first language models, and he did it by counting. Take a large body of text, and for every pair of words, count how often the second follows the first. Now, to generate text, look at the last word you wrote, look up everything that ever followed it, and choose one in proportion to how often it did. This is called a bigram model. If you look back two words rather than one, it is a trigram model, and in general these are called n-gram models.\n\nSuch models produce text that is locally plausible and globally demented. Each phrase of three or four words is one that a person might write; but the sentence wanders off without ever arriving anywhere, because the model has no memory of anything further back than a few words. On the companion site there is a demonstration that lets you build these models from a sample of text and watch the output become more coherent as you lengthen the memory.\n\nSo why not just lengthen it? Look back twenty words instead of two. The problem is the one that statisticians call the curse of dimensionality. The number of possible sequences of twenty words is unimaginably vast, and almost none of them has ever been written. If your model works by looking up what followed this exact sequence in the past, then for almost any twenty-word sequence the answer is that there is no past: it has never occurred before. Counting can only tell you about what you have seen.\n\nAnd notice what a counting model does not know. Suppose it has seen the sentence \"the cat is sleeping on the sofa\" many times. It has learned nothing whatever from that about \"the dog is sleeping on the sofa\" or \"a kitten was napping on the couch\". To the counting model, \"cat\" and \"dog\" are simply two different symbols, as unrelated as \"cat\" and \"carburettor\". It has no notion that words can be similar to one another. For it to generalise, it would need some representation of words in which similar words are close together.\n\nN-gram models were nevertheless the workhorse of speech recognition and machine translation for some forty years, and I should not be too rude about them. But to get past them, the field needed an answer to a question that sounds easy and is not. How do you feed a word to a neural network?\n\nTokens.\n\nA network takes in numbers. Text is made of symbols. So the first step, always, is to chop the text into pieces, and assign each piece a number.\n\nThe pieces are called tokens. You might guess that a token is a word, and in early systems it was. But words are awkward. There are too many of them; new ones are invented daily; and what should the system do with a misspelling, or a name, or a string of computer code? You could instead use individual characters, which solves all those problems, but then even a short paragraph is a very long sequence, and the network must spend much of its effort simply learning to spell.\n\nModern systems split the difference. Their tokens are fragments, somewhere between a letter and a word, which are chosen automatically by an algorithm that scans a large body of text and merges the most frequent combinations of characters, again and again, until it has built up a vocabulary of the desired size. Typically that is somewhere from fifty thousand to a couple of hundred thousand tokens. Common words get a token to themselves. Rare words are assembled from pieces; \"tokenisation\" might come out as \"token\", then \"isation\". Anything at all can be represented, in the worst case by spelling it out letter by letter.\n\nAs a rule of thumb for English, one token is about three-quarters of a word. And tokens are the unit in which nearly everything about language models is measured. The amount of text a model can take into account at once, called its context window, is counted in tokens. So is the size of the training data, and so is the price that the companies charge. When someone says that a model was trained on ten trillion tokens, or that it has a context window of a million tokens, you can now translate.\n\nFrom symbols to geometry.\n\nSo now each token is a number, an index into the vocabulary. Token number 3,797, say. But that number is just a label. Token 3,797 is not in any meaningful sense close to token 3,798. We are back to the problem of \"cat\" and \"carburettor\".\n\nThe solution is one of the most consequential ideas in the whole field, and it is an idea a physicist will find natural. Represent each token not as a label, but as a point in a space. Give every token in the vocabulary its own vector: a list of a few hundred, or a few thousand, numbers. These vectors are called embeddings, because each word is embedded as a point in a high-dimensional space.\n\nWhere do the numbers in these vectors come from? This is the elegant part. They are weights. They begin as random numbers, and they are learned, by backpropagation, in just the same way as every other weight in the network. The complete set of embeddings is simply a big table, with one row for each token in the vocabulary, and the first thing the network does with a token is to look up its row. During training, the gradient flows all the way back to that table and nudges each word's vector in whichever direction makes the predictions better.\n\nThink about what that pressure does. If \"cat\" and \"dog\" tend to be followed by similar words, and they do: \"is sleeping\", \"wants to go out\", \"needs to go to the vet\", then the network makes better predictions if it treats them alike, and the cheapest way to treat them alike is to give them similar vectors. So, over the course of training, words that are used in similar ways drift together in the space. Nobody tells the network that cats and dogs are both animals. It moves them close because that helps with the guessing game.\n\nThis idea was set out in a 2003 paper by Yoshua Bengio and his colleagues in Montreal. Linguists had, in a sense, anticipated it half a century before. It is called the distributional hypothesis, and its best-known statement is from the English linguist John Firth in 1957: you shall know a word by the company it keeps.\n\nKing minus man plus woman.\n\nThe idea became famous a decade later. In 2013, a team at Google led by a Czech researcher named Tomas Mikolov published a method called word2vec. It was a deliberately simple and stripped-down neural network, hardly deep at all. Its one task was to take a word and predict the words that appear near it. Its simplicity meant that it was fast, fast enough to be trained on billions of words of text, and when the training was done, you threw away the network and kept the embeddings.\n\nThose embeddings had a property that astonished people. Similar words were close together, as expected. But beyond that, the directions in the space carried meaning. Take the vector for \"king\". Subtract from it the vector for \"man\". Add the vector for \"woman\". Then ask which word's vector is closest to the point where you have arrived. The answer is \"queen\".\n\nIt works for other relationships too. Paris minus France plus Italy lands near Rome. \"Walking\" minus \"walk\" plus \"swim\" lands near \"swimming\". What this means is that there is a consistent direction in the space that corresponds to the difference between male and female, and another for the relation of a capital to its country, and another for a verb's tense. Concepts have become directions. Relationships between meanings have become geometric relationships between vectors, and you can do arithmetic on them.\n\nNobody designed the space to have this structure. It fell out of gradient descent, applied to nothing more than the task of predicting which words turn up near which. The examples were somewhat cherry-picked, I should say, and the arithmetic does not always work so neatly. But the central finding has held up and has only grown in importance: neural networks represent concepts as directions in a high-dimensional space.\n\nIt is worth pausing over the high-dimensional part, since it is where our intuitions are least reliable. In three dimensions, you can have just three directions that are all at right angles to one another. In a thousand dimensions you can have a thousand. But there is a much stranger fact about high-dimensional spaces. If you are willing to accept directions that are only nearly perpendicular, at eighty-five degrees to each other, say, rather than ninety, then the number of them that you can fit in is not a thousand, but grows exponentially with the dimension. A space of a few thousand dimensions has room for many millions of almost independent directions. So an embedding vector is not a list of a thousand features. It can be a compressed encoding of vastly more. This fact will turn out to be essential when, in chapter twelve, we look at attempts to read what is going on inside these networks.\n\nThe free lunch of self-supervision.\n\nThere is something about word2vec that is easy to pass over, and it may be the most important thing in this chapter. Where did the training labels come from?\n\nRecall ImageNet. It worked because of an enormous effort by tens of thousands of human beings to label millions of pictures by hand. That kind of learning, from examples that people have labelled with the right answers, is called supervised learning, and its bottleneck is plain: human labour.\n\nNow look at the guessing game. The task is to predict the next word. The right answer is the next word. It is sitting right there in the text. No one has to label anything. Every sentence that anyone has ever written is a supply of training examples, complete with answers, free of charge. This is called self-supervised learning: the data supervises itself.\n\nThe significance of this is hard to overstate. It means that the supply of training data for language is, in effect, the whole of the internet, and every digitised book, and every scientific paper, and all the publicly posted computer code. That is trillions of words. And the training objective that unlocks all of it is the one Shannon was playing with in 1950. In the last chapter I said that the choice of what question to ask the network might be the most powerful lever of all. This is what I meant. The question \"what comes next?\" needs no labels, and has no ceiling, in the sense that you can always get better at it by understanding more.\n\nTurning scores into probabilities.\n\nThere are two more pieces of machinery to put in place, both at the output end of the network. Each of them will be familiar to a physicist, wearing an unfamiliar name.\n\nA language model has to output a probability for every token in its vocabulary. But what the final layer of a network naturally produces is just a list of numbers, one per token, which can be anything: positive or negative, large or small. Those raw scores are called logits. To turn them into probabilities, you need to make them all positive, and make them sum to one. The standard way to do that is to take the exponential of each score, and then divide by the sum of all the exponentials. This operation is called the softmax.\n\nIf you have studied statistical mechanics, you have seen that formula before. It is the Boltzmann distribution. The logits play the part of negative energies, and the sum in the denominator is the partition function. And the parallel goes further. In physics, there is a temperature in the exponent, which controls how strongly the system prefers its low-energy states. Language models have just the same thing, and it is even called temperature. Before applying the softmax, you divide all of the logits by a temperature. At a low temperature the distribution sharpens, and the model nearly always chooses its single most probable token; its output becomes predictable and repetitive. At a high temperature the distribution flattens, less likely tokens get their chance, and the output becomes more surprising, more creative and, eventually, incoherent. When a developer adjusts the temperature setting on a language model, that is literally what is being adjusted.\n\nThe second piece is the loss function. The model has produced a probability distribution over the next token. We know which token actually came next. How wrong was the model? The standard answer is to look at the probability that the model gave to the correct token, and take the negative of its logarithm. If the model gave the right answer a probability of one, the loss is zero. If it gave the right answer a probability of one in a thousand, the loss is large. This is the cross-entropy loss, and it is the quantity that nearly every large language model is trained to minimise.\n\nIt has a nice interpretation, which brings us back to Shannon. The negative logarithm of a probability is a measure of surprise: how many bits of information you gain on learning that the thing happened. So the cross-entropy loss is the model's average surprise at the actual text. Training a language model means making it, on average, as unsurprised as possible by what human beings write. It is also, exactly, the number of bits per token that you would need to compress the text using the model's predictions. Prediction and compression are the same thing. A good language model is a good compressor of human writing, and the strongest argument that it must have learnt something about the world is that you cannot compress what you do not, in some sense, understand.\n\nWhat a single vector cannot do.\n\nSo by 2013 the field had a way to turn words into vectors that capture meaning, and an inexhaustible source of training signal. What it did not yet have was a good way to deal with words in combination.\n\nThe trouble with word2vec is that it gives one vector to each word, and words do not work like that. The word \"bank\" in \"river bank\" and in \"bank account\" would get the same vector, a muddy average of two unrelated meanings. The word \"it\" means nothing at all until you know what it refers to. Meaning depends on context: on the other words in the sentence, on their order, and on words that may be a long way off. \"The trophy would not fit in the suitcase because it was too big.\" What was too big? You know at once that it was the trophy. Change \"big\" to \"small\", and \"it\" becomes the suitcase. To work that out, you have to combine information from across the whole sentence, along with some knowledge about how objects fit inside other objects.\n\nWhat was needed was a network that reads a sequence of word vectors and produces representations of the words in context. For a quarter of a century, the leading candidate for that job was a design called the recurrent neural network. Its struggles, and the workaround that grew into its replacement, are the subject of the next chapter.\n Chapter 6: The Memory Problem.\n\nNetworks with a loop.\n\nThe networks we have met so far take in an input of fixed size, a picture of so many pixels, and produce an output. Information flows in one direction, from bottom to top, and then the computation is over. Language does not come like that. A sentence can be three words long or three hundred. It arrives in order, and the order matters. \"Dog bites man\" is not news; \"man bites dog\" is.\n\nThe natural way to process a sequence is the way you are processing this one: one item at a time, keeping a running memory of what has come before. The network design built on that idea is called the recurrent neural network, or RNN. The idea goes back to the nineteen-eighties.\n\nHere is how it works. The network maintains a vector called the hidden state. Think of it as the network's working memory, a few hundred numbers that summarise everything that it has read so far. At each step, the network takes two things: the embedding of the next word, and its own hidden state from the previous step. It combines them, through the usual matrix multiplication and non-linearity, and produces a new hidden state. Then it goes on to the next word and does the same again, with the same weights. Read a word, update the memory. Read a word, update the memory. When the end of the sentence is reached, the final hidden state is, in principle, a summary of all of it. And at any point, you can attach an output layer to the hidden state and ask it to predict the next word.\n\nIt is an appealing design. It handles sequences of any length. It builds in a sensible inductive bias: the same rules apply at each position in the sequence, in much the same way as a convolution applies the same filter at each position in an image. And there is at least a passing resemblance to what it feels like to read.\n\nForgetting.\n\nIn practice, plain recurrent networks did not work well, and you already have the tools to see why.\n\nHow do you train one? You use backpropagation, as always. But consider the path that the error signal must take. Suppose the network makes a mistake at the fiftieth word of a sentence, and the information that it needed was at the third word. To correct the weights, the error signal has to travel backwards from step fifty to step three, through forty-seven successive applications of the same update. In effect, a recurrent network processing a fifty-word sentence is a network that is fifty layers deep, with one layer for each time step.\n\nAnd we know what happens to gradients in very deep networks. They vanish. The signal is multiplied by a similar factor at each step back, and it shrinks exponentially. It was precisely this setting that Sepp Hochreiter was analysing in the 1991 thesis I mentioned in chapter three. The practical upshot is that a plain RNN can learn connections between words that are five or ten steps apart, and little beyond that. It forgets. The start of a long sentence has faded from its memory by the time it reaches the end, and worse, it cannot learn to remember, because no training signal reaches back that far.\n\nHochreiter did not just diagnose the illness. In 1997, with his supervisor Jürgen Schmidhuber, he published a cure. They called it long short-term memory, or LSTM. The name is odd, but the meaning is: a short-term memory that lasts for a long time.\n\nThe insight was to change the way that memory gets updated. In a plain RNN, the entire hidden state is rewritten at each step; it is pushed through a matrix and a squashing function, and comes out scrambled. In an LSTM there is a separate channel, called the cell state, that runs along the whole sequence like a conveyor belt. By default, what is on the belt just stays there, carried from each step to the next without being altered. The network changes it only by means of small, deliberate, additive edits, and these are controlled by components called gates. A gate is a little sub-network that outputs numbers between zero and one, which serve as dimmer switches. One gate decides what to erase from the belt. One decides what new information to write to it. One decides how much of it to reveal to the rest of the network at this step. And all of the gates are learned.\n\nYou may spot the family resemblance. Because the memory is carried forward by addition, and is not repeatedly transformed, gradients can flow back along the conveyor belt across many time steps without vanishing. It is the same trick as the residual connection from chapter four, an express lane for gradients, and the LSTM had it eighteen years earlier. Schmidhuber, I should mention, has been arguing for decades that he and his students have received too little credit for this and several other ideas, and on this particular point he is clearly right.\n\nLike so much else, the LSTM lay almost unused for a decade and more, awaiting data and computing power. When those arrived, it took over. By around 2015, LSTMs were behind the speech recognition in your phone, the predictive text on your keyboard, and a growing share of machine translation. That year, a doctoral student at Stanford named Andrej Karpathy wrote a widely read blog post titled \"The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Recurrent Neural Networks\", in which he trained LSTMs to predict text one character at a time. Fed on Shakespeare, the network turned out passable mock-Shakespeare, with speaker names and stage directions. Fed on the source code of the Linux operating system, it wrote things that looked like computer code, with correctly matched brackets and plausible comments. The output was nonsense, but it was nonsense with a structure, and it gave many people their first inkling that a network trained to do nothing but predict the next symbol could pick up a surprising amount about the form of what it was reading.\n\nTranslation as a test case.\n\nThe problem that drove the next breakthroughs was machine translation. It was a good problem: commercially valuable, hard, and well supplied with data, since the proceedings of the European and Canadian parliaments and the documents of the United Nations furnished millions of sentences, each carefully translated by professionals.\n\nThe dominant approach in 2014 was called statistical machine translation. Systems of this kind were prodigious feats of engineering: pipelines of many separate components, tables of phrases and their translations, n-gram language models, rules for reordering words, each one built and tuned by large teams over years. Google Translate was such a system.\n\nThat year, a group at Google that included Ilya Sutskever, lately arrived from Toronto, proposed to replace the whole pipeline with one neural network. Their paper was called \"Sequence to Sequence Learning with Neural Networks\", and the design was almost provocatively simple. Take two LSTMs. The first, called the encoder, reads the English sentence one word at a time. When it gets to the end, its hidden state is a single vector, a few thousand numbers, which is supposed to capture the meaning of the entire sentence. Hand that vector over to the second LSTM, called the decoder, as its initial memory. The decoder then produces the French translation one word at a time, each word conditioned on that vector and on the words it has written so far.\n\nSo: a whole sentence is squeezed down into one vector, and a whole sentence is unpacked from it. No phrase tables, no linguistic rules, no alignment of words. Just pairs of sentences, a loss function, and gradient descent. With a big enough network, it matched the carefully engineered systems. In a talk he gave at the time, Sutskever stated his creed with a bluntness that people still quote: if you have a large dataset, and you train a very big neural network, then success is guaranteed.\n\nThe bottleneck.\n\nThe design had an obvious weak point, and you have probably seen it already. Everything that the decoder knows about the source sentence has to pass through that one vector. It is a bottleneck. A short sentence may fit into it. A fifty-word sentence with three subordinate clauses will not, and indeed, the quality of the translations dropped off sharply as the sentences grew longer. A computational linguist named Ray Mooney expressed the general scepticism in a line that became famous, which I shall paraphrase for politeness: you cannot cram the meaning of a whole sentence into a single vector.\n\nThink about how a human translator works. You do not read a long sentence, close the book, and then write the translation from memory. You keep the original in front of you, and as you write each word of the translation, your eyes go back to the part of the source that is relevant at that moment.\n\nTowards the end of 2014, a paper appeared that gave the network the ability to do just this. It came out of Yoshua Bengio's lab in Montreal, and its first author, Dzmitry Bahdanau, was an intern there. The mechanism it introduced was given the name attention.\n\nAttention.\n\nHere is the idea. Let the encoder read the source sentence as before; but do not throw away its intermediate states. Keep all of them: one vector for every word of the source, each representing that word in its context. Now, when the decoder is about to produce the next word of the translation, let it look back over all of those source vectors and decide which of them matter at this moment.\n\nSpecifically, the decoder takes its own current state, which you can think of as expressing \"this is what I am trying to write next\", and compares it with each of the source vectors in turn. Each comparison yields a score: how relevant is that source word to what I am doing now? The scores are passed through a softmax, the same function from the last chapter, which turns them into a set of positive weights that add up to one. Then the decoder takes a weighted average of the source vectors, using those weights. Source words with high scores contribute a lot to the average, and those with low scores contribute almost nothing. The result is a single vector, a custom-made summary of the source sentence, tailored to the needs of this particular step. For the next word of output, the decoder asks again, and gets a different summary.\n\nSo the fixed bottleneck has gone. In its place the network has something like a spotlight, which it can move across the source sentence, to light up whichever part it needs.\n\nThree things about this mechanism are worth dwelling on, because everything that follows is built on them.\n\nThe first is that it is soft. The network does not pick out one source word to look at. It takes a blend of all of them, in which some count for much more than others. That matters a great deal, for a technical reason. A hard choice, look at word three and ignore the rest, is not differentiable; you cannot take the gradient of a decision. A weighted average is a smooth function of its weights, and the weights are smooth functions of the scores. So gradients flow through the whole thing, and the network can learn where to look by ordinary backpropagation. Nobody tells it which source words go with which target words. It works that out, because looking in the right place reduces the loss.\n\nThe second is that attention is content-based. The network retrieves information according to what it is, not according to where it is. The decoder does not say \"give me the fourth word\". It says, in effect, \"give me whatever is relevant to what I am writing now\", and it finds it wherever in the sentence it happens to be. If you have a computing background, think of it as a soft version of a database lookup. There is a query, which is what I am looking for. There are keys, which describe what each stored item has to offer. And there are values, the items themselves. An ordinary lookup returns the one value whose key matches the query exactly. Attention returns a blend of all the values, each weighted by how well its key matches the query. Query, key and value: hold on to those three words, because in the next chapter they become the central machinery.\n\nThe third is that distance no longer matters. In a recurrent network, information from the start of a sentence has to survive a journey through every intervening step to be of use at the end. With attention, the decoder can reach directly to any position in the source in a single hop. The first word is no further away than the last.\n\nAnd as a bonus, you can see what the network is doing. Plot the attention weights as a grid, with source words along one side and target words along the other, and you get a picture of which source words the network was looking at as it wrote each target word. For English and French, the bright cells run mostly along the diagonal, since the two languages have a similar word order. But where French puts the adjective after the noun, as in \"zone économique européenne\" for \"European economic area\", the bright cells neatly reverse themselves. The network had found out how the words of the two languages line up with one another, and no one had told it to.\n\nThe great awakening.\n\nThe effect on translation was immediate. Within two years, attention-equipped neural networks had overtaken the statistical systems that had been refined over the previous two decades.\n\nIn the autumn of 2016, Google replaced the system behind Google Translate with a neural one: deep stacks of LSTMs, an encoder and a decoder, connected by attention. The switch was first made, without any announcement, for translation between Japanese and English, and some users in Japan noticed overnight that the translations had gone from stilted to fluent, and wondered what had happened. By Google's own measurements, the new system reduced translation errors by more than half for several pairs of languages. It was a bigger improvement in a single step than the old system had made in the whole of the previous decade. A long article about the project in the New York Times Magazine at the end of that year was entitled \"The Great A.I. Awakening\".\n\nIt also said something about the economics of research. The old system had been the work of hundreds of engineers and linguists over many years. The new one was built by a much smaller group in about nine months, and its code was a fraction of the length. All of that accumulated linguistic engineering had been replaced by one generic learning machine and a mountain of data. This pattern has a name, which we will come to in chapter nine.\n\nWhat was still wrong.\n\nSo by the end of 2016, the state of the art for language was the LSTM with attention. It worked. But the people who were building these systems were up against a limit, and it was not a limit of accuracy. It was a limit of speed.\n\nA recurrent network is sequential by nature. To compute the hidden state at word fifty, you need the hidden state at word forty-nine, and for that you need word forty-eight. There is no way of doing the steps out of order, and no way of doing them at the same time. And this is exactly what a GPU is bad at. A GPU gets its power from doing thousands of things at once. Give it a long chain of calculations, each of which has to wait for the one before, and most of that vast parallel machine sits idle.\n\nCompare that with the convolutional networks that had triumphed in vision. A convolution applies the same filter at every position of an image independently, so all the positions can be computed simultaneously. Vision networks could soak up as much parallel hardware as you could give them. Language networks could not. And since, as we have seen, the route to better performance was bigger networks and more data, that was a serious problem. Training was taking weeks. The architecture was holding back the scale.\n\nA few people at Google began to ask a question that sounds, with hindsight, like the obvious one. In these systems, the LSTM was there to carry information along the sequence, and attention had been bolted on to help. But attention could already carry information between any two positions directly, and it could do so for all positions at once. Was the recurrent part really doing anything essential? What would happen if you took it out, and built a network from nothing but attention?\n\nThe paper that answered that question was published in June 2017. Its title gave the answer away. It was called \"Attention Is All You Need\".\n Chapter 7: Attention Is All You Need.\n\nEight authors.\n\nThe most influential paper in the modern history of artificial intelligence was written by eight people at Google, in a few hectic months in the first half of 2017. Its first page carries a footnote which says that all eight contributed equally and that the order of their names is random. That is unusual, and it gives you a sense of how the thing came about: not as the plan of one senior scientist, but as a collaboration that snowballed, picking up people in corridors.\n\nThe seed was planted by Jakob Uszkoreit, a researcher whose father happened to be a well-known computational linguist. Uszkoreit had become convinced that the attention mechanism might be enough by itself to handle language, without any recurrent network underneath, and he had a name ready for a model that would transform its representations step by step. He would call it the Transformer. Two colleagues, Ashish Vaswani and Illia Polosukhin, joined him in trying to make it work. Niki Parmar and Llion Jones came aboard, and then Łukasz Kaiser, and his intern Aidan Gomez, who was twenty years old and still an undergraduate student at the University of Toronto.\n\nThe early versions worked, but did not beat the LSTMs. What turned the corner, by all accounts, was the arrival of Noam Shazeer. Shazeer was something of a legend inside Google; he had joined the company in 2000 and built, among other things, the system that asks \"did you mean\" when you misspell a search. He overheard a conversation about the project, decided that it was the most promising thing he had come across in years, and rewrote the whole code base. His version was faster and cleaner, and then the team started taking parts out to see what would break. Remarkably little did. The simpler they made the model, the better it performed.\n\nThe title was Llion Jones's contribution, a nod to the Beatles song \"All You Need Is Love\". The paper was posted in June 2017, and presented that December at the field's main conference. It reported that the Transformer had set a new record for the quality of machine translation between English and German, and between English and French. And it had got there with a small fraction of the training cost of its rivals: three and a half days, on eight GPUs.\n\nNobody involved thought they had found a route to machines that could pass the bar exam. It was a paper about translation. Within a few years, all eight authors had left Google, most of them to found companies, and the architecture they described had become the foundation of very nearly everything.\n\nLet us take it apart. I am going to describe the version that is used in modern language models, which is a little simpler than the original, and I will describe it in more detail than anything else in this book, because once you have this, you have the central object of the field.\n\nThe setting.\n\nStart with what goes in and what comes out. What goes in is a sequence of tokens: the text so far. What comes out is a probability distribution for the token that comes next.\n\nThe first step is the one from chapter five. Each token is looked up in the embedding table and replaced by its vector. So if the text is a thousand tokens long, we now have a thousand vectors, one sitting at each position. In a large modern model, each of those vectors might be a list of several thousand numbers.\n\nHere is the mental picture I would like you to hold. Imagine those thousand vectors as a thousand columns standing side by side, one for each token. The network consists of a stack of layers, and as we move up through the layers, each column's vector gets progressively revised. At the bottom, the vector at a given position just represents the token there, on its own, out of context: the word \"bank\", in all its ambiguity. By the time we reach the top, that vector has been enriched with information from all over the text. It now represents that token in its context, and more than that, it contains whatever is needed to predict what follows.\n\nEach layer does two things to these columns, one after the other. First, it lets the columns exchange information with each other. That is attention. Second, it lets each column, separately, think about what it has gathered. That is an ordinary multi-layer perceptron. Communicate, then compute. Then the next layer does it all again. That is the whole of the architecture. The rest is details, though some of the details are clever.\n\nSelf-attention.\n\nIn the last chapter, attention was a bridge between two networks: the decoder attended to the encoder's states. The Transformer uses the same mechanism for a different purpose. It lets the positions in a single sequence attend to one another. This is called self-attention, and it is how the columns communicate.\n\nRemember the vocabulary of query, key and value. In self-attention, every position plays all three roles at once.\n\nTake a particular position: the word \"it\", in the sentence \"the trophy would not fit in the suitcase because it was too big\". The vector at that position is multiplied by a matrix of learned weights to produce a new, smaller vector: its query. You can think of the query as a broadcast question. For a pronoun, the question might be something like: \"I am looking for a noun, singular, a thing and not a person, mentioned earlier\".\n\nMeanwhile, the vector at each position is multiplied by a second learned matrix, to give that position's key. The key is a sort of advertisement: this is the kind of information I have to offer. The key at the position of \"trophy\" might say, in effect: \"singular noun, physical object, subject of the sentence\".\n\nNow compare the query from \"it\" with the key from every position. The comparison is a dot product, the same measure of alignment that we met in the very first chapter. Where the query and the key point in similar directions, the dot product is large: this position has what I want. Where they are unrelated, it is close to zero. That gives a score for every position in the sequence. Put the scores through a softmax, and they become a set of weights that sum to one. Perhaps sixty per cent on \"trophy\", thirty on \"suitcase\", and scraps on everything else.\n\nFinally, each position's vector is multiplied by a third learned matrix, to produce its value. The value is the actual content that it will hand over if it is attended to. Take the average of all the values, weighted by the attention weights. That weighted average is the answer to the query. And it is added to the vector at the position of \"it\". The column for \"it\" now contains some trophy-ness, which it did not contain before.\n\nThat is one attention operation, for one position. The same thing happens at every position simultaneously. Every token sends out a query, every token advertises a key, every token offers a value, and each one's vector is updated with a blend of the others'.\n\nI have dramatised it by putting words in the mouths of the queries and the keys, and I should be clear that this is a cartoon. Nobody writes those questions and advertisements. A query is just a vector, the product of a learned matrix and the vector in the column. The three matrices that produce queries, keys and values begin as random numbers, and are shaped by gradient descent like every other weight. All that training cares about is that the predictions at the end come out right. Whatever questions it is useful for the tokens to put to one another, the network will learn to ask them.\n\nBut here is what I want you to appreciate about this mechanism. In an ordinary neural network layer, the pattern of connections is fixed: this neuron is wired to those neurons, with these weights, and that is that, whatever the input. In attention, the pattern of who talks to whom is computed afresh for each input. The wiring depends on the data. For one sentence, position ten draws on position three; for another, it draws on position seven. It is this dynamic, content-dependent routing of information that gives the Transformer its flexibility.\n\nMany heads.\n\nOne round of attention lets each token ask one kind of question. But there are many relationships in a sentence that matter at once. A verb may need to find its subject, and also its object, and also the adverb that modifies it. A pronoun needs its antecedent. A closing bracket needs its opening bracket.\n\nSo the Transformer runs several attention operations side by side, within each layer. Each has its own three matrices, and so its own sort of query, key and value. These are called attention heads. The original Transformer had eight in each layer; large modern models have dozens. Each head attends to the sequence in its own way, and the results of all of them are added into the columns.\n\nWhen researchers went to look at what individual heads had learnt to do, they found a certain amount of intelligible specialisation. There are heads that attend from each word to the one before. There are heads that link verbs to their objects, and heads that link pronouns to the nouns they stand for. There are heads that seem to follow which bracket or quotation mark is currently open. And there are many heads whose behaviour no one has been able to put a name to.\n\nCompute: the MLP.\n\nAfter the attention step, each column holds a blend of information that has been gathered from elsewhere. The second half of the layer processes it. This part is very plain. It is a small multi-layer perceptron, of the kind we met in chapter three: a matrix multiplication that expands the vector to something like four times its width, a non-linearity, and a second matrix multiplication that brings it back down.\n\nThe MLP is applied to each position separately. At this stage there is no communication between columns; that was the job of attention. Each column simply digests what it has received. And the result, once again, is added to the column's vector.\n\nIt would be easy to take these MLP blocks for an afterthought, but they hold about two-thirds of the weights in a typical Transformer. The best current evidence is that they are where much of the model's factual knowledge resides. An attention head might pull together the information that the sentence is about the Eiffel Tower, and that what is wanted is a location; it is then an MLP that responds to that combination by writing \"Paris\" into the column. One way to think about them is as an enormous associative memory: when this pattern shows up, add that.\n\nThe residual stream.\n\nYou will have noticed that I keep saying \"added\". The output of attention is added to the column. The output of the MLP is added to the column. These are the residual connections from chapter four, and in the Transformer they are so central that people who study these models have a name for the running total. They call it the residual stream.\n\nHere is the picture. Each column is a channel that runs straight up through the network from bottom to top. The token's embedding is put into it at the bottom. Then every attention head and every MLP, at every layer, reads from that channel and writes back into it by addition. No component ever overwrites the stream. Each one just adds its contribution to what is already there. At the top, whatever has accumulated in the stream is read off to make the prediction.\n\nThis is a different picture from the one we began with in chapter three, where each layer replaces its input with a brand new representation. Here, the layers are more like a succession of editors making notes on a shared document. An early layer may write \"this token is a noun\". A middle layer may read that, and write \"and it is the subject of the verb 'fit'\". A late layer may read both of those, and write \"so the next word is probably an adjective describing size\". All of the notes pile up in the same vector, which has room for them, because, as we saw in chapter five, a space of several thousand dimensions can hold a very great number of nearly independent directions.\n\nThe residual stream is also what makes the network trainable at depth, for the reason given in chapter four. Gradients flow straight down the stream from the output to every layer, without interruption. That is why Transformers can be stacked a hundred layers deep without the gradients vanishing. A normalisation step is also applied before each component, to keep the numbers in a sensible range.\n\nWhere am I? The problem of position.\n\nThere is something odd about self-attention as I have described it. It has no sense of word order.\n\nLook at the mechanism again. Each position's query is compared with each position's key, and the comparison depends only on the content of the vectors. Nothing in it knows that one token is the third in the sequence and another is the tenth. If you shuffled the words of the sentence, every token would receive exactly the same blend of information as before. To raw self-attention, a sentence is a bag of words. And that will not do; we began the last chapter with \"dog bites man\".\n\nA recurrent network gets word order for nothing, since it reads the words in order. The Transformer gave that up when it gave up recurrence, and so position has to be put back in by hand. The method is to stamp each token with its position before it enters the network. Along with the embedding that says what the token is, you add a second vector that says where it is.\n\nIn the original paper, this was done in a way a physicist will enjoy. Each position was encoded as a set of sine and cosine waves of different frequencies, from very fast to very slow, sampled at that position. It is rather like the way the hands of a clock encode the time: a second hand, a minute hand and an hour hand, each sweeping round at its own rate, which together identify a moment uniquely. It works, because the relative offset between any two positions corresponds to a fixed rotation of each pair of sine and cosine components, and that is something a dot product can easily detect. Modern models use more refined schemes, the most popular of which rotates the query and key vectors by an angle that depends on their position. But the principle is unchanged: order is not built into the architecture, but supplied as additional information in the data.\n\nThat is a striking design choice, when you think about it. The Transformer is not, fundamentally, a model of sequences. It is a model of sets of vectors that can exchange information with one another, and the fact that they come in a sequence is just one more feature that those vectors may carry. That turns out to matter enormously, as we shall see in a moment.\n\nNo peeking.\n\nOne further detail is needed to turn this into a language model, and it is the key to how efficiently these models can be trained.\n\nWe are training the network to predict the next token. So when it is working out its prediction for what follows position ten, it had better not be able to see position eleven. That would be cheating. The fix is easy: in the attention step, each position is allowed to attend only to itself and to earlier positions. Attention to later positions is blocked; their scores are set to negative infinity before the softmax, so that their weights are zero. This is known as causal masking. Information flows only from the past to the future.\n\nAnd here is the benefit. Since each position sees only its own past, the column at position ten makes a legitimate prediction of token eleven, and the column at position eleven makes a legitimate prediction of token twelve, and so on, all in the same forward pass. Feed in a passage of a thousand tokens, and you get a thousand predictions back, each of which can be marked against the token that actually came next. A thousand training examples for the price of one, computed in parallel. Compare this with the recurrent network, which had to creep through the passage one step at a time.\n\nWhen the model is generating text, on the other hand, it still has to go one token at a time, as there is nothing yet in the future to predict from: generate a token, append it, run the network again. That is why your chatbot types its answer out. But training is massively parallel, and training is where the expense lies.\n\nTo finish, the top of the stack. After the final layer, the vector in each column is multiplied by one last matrix, which gives a score for every token in the vocabulary. Those are the logits. Softmax turns them into probabilities, and the cross-entropy loss marks them against the truth. Backpropagation then carries the gradient down through every layer, into every head and every MLP, into the embedding table. And that completes the machine.\n\nWhy it mattered.\n\nSo, why was this paper so important? There were other successful architectures in 2017. What was it about this one?\n\nThe first answer is parallelism. I said at the end of the last chapter that the recurrent network was a poor fit for the GPU. The Transformer is a perfect fit. Every position is processed at the same time. The whole computation is big matrix multiplications from top to bottom. That meant that for the first time, a language model could make full use of as much hardware as you could afford. The Transformer was not just a better model at a given size. It was a model that you could make a thousand times bigger and still be able to train.\n\nThe second answer is that it removed the problem of distance. Any token can draw on any other token directly, in one step, however far apart they are. Long-range dependency, the thing that recurrent networks fought with for twenty-five years, is simply not a difficulty.\n\nThe third answer is generality, and this goes back to that point about sets. The Transformer has very little inductive bias. It does not assume that its input is a sentence, or an image, or anything in particular. It assumes only that the input is a collection of vectors, and that they may need to share information. So you can use it for anything that can be chopped into pieces and turned into vectors. Chop an image into small square patches, treat every patch as a token, and you have a vision model that eventually came to outperform convolutional networks. Do the same with slices of audio, and you have speech recognition. With the amino acids in a protein, and you have the core of AlphaFold, which solved the problem of predicting protein structure and earned its makers a Nobel Prize. Before 2017, each of those fields had its own specialised architectures and its own community. Since then they have converged on one. One researcher, Andrej Karpathy, has described the Transformer as a general-purpose computer that happens to be differentiable, and so can be programmed by gradient descent in place of a programmer.\n\nThere is a cost, and you should know about it. Since every token attends to every other, the amount of computation grows as the square of the length of the sequence. Double the length of the text, and the attention step costs four times as much. This is the main reason that models have a limited context window, and a great deal of engineering effort has gone into pushing that limit from the few hundred tokens of 2017 to the million or more of today.\n\nWhat is perhaps most remarkable of all is how little has changed since. If you were to open up one of the frontier models of today, you would find an architecture which the eight authors would recognise immediately. There have been adjustments: where the normalisation goes, how position is encoded, how the attention calculation is organised to save memory. But the design is the one from 2017: columns of vectors, layers of attention and MLPs, a residual stream. Nearly all the progress since then has come from somewhere else. It has come from what the model is trained on, and how, and above all from how big it is.\n\nThe people who grasped that first were not at Google. They were at a small research laboratory in San Francisco, where one of the founders was a man we have already met twice, and who believed that a large enough network, trained on enough data, would simply work. That laboratory was OpenAI, and the man was Ilya Sutskever.\n Chapter 8: The Generative Pre-trained Transformer.\n\nA neuron for sentiment.\n\nIn 2017, the year of the Transformer paper, a young researcher at OpenAI named Alec Radford ran an experiment whose result surprised him. He took eighty-two million product reviews from Amazon, and trained a recurrent network on them to do one thing only: to predict the next character. Not the next word; the next letter or punctuation mark. It took a month.\n\nAfterwards he examined the network to see what, if anything, it had learnt. Among its four thousand or so neurons he found one that was behaving in a remarkable way. Its value went up when the review was favourable, and down when it was unfavourable. Reading through a review a character at a time, that neuron kept a running assessment of the writer's opinion. When a review began with praise and then turned sour, with a \"however\", the neuron swung from positive to negative just at that point. And reading off that one neuron's value gave a better classifier of sentiment than systems that had been purpose-built and trained for the task.\n\nNobody had told the network anything about sentiment. No review had been labelled as positive or negative. The network had only been asked to guess the next character. But think about what helps with that. If you know that the reviewer is pleased, then you know that the next word is more likely to be \"excellent\" than \"broken\". An internal variable that keeps track of the reviewer's mood is useful for prediction, and so gradient descent had built one.\n\nThis was a small result, but to the people at OpenAI it pointed at something big. If predicting text forces a network to discover sentiment, without being asked to, what else might it be forced to discover? Grammar, certainly. Facts about the world, perhaps. Perhaps the rudiments of reasoning. And what if you ran the same experiment with a better architecture, on much more text?\n\nThe laboratory.\n\nOpenAI had been founded at the end of 2015 by a group that included Sam Altman, who at the time ran the start-up incubator Y Combinator; Elon Musk; and Ilya Sutskever, who was lured away from Google to be its chief scientist. It was set up as a non-profit, with a billion dollars in pledges and a mission statement about ensuring that artificial general intelligence would benefit all of humanity. Behind this lay a fear, shared by its founders, that this technology was going to be very powerful, and that it ought not to be left in the hands of Google alone.\n\nFor its first couple of years the lab was not notably focused on language. It worked on programs that learned to play video games, and on a robot hand. Radford's work on language models was a side project. But when the Transformer paper appeared, he and Sutskever saw straight away what it meant for the experiment they wanted to do. Here was an architecture that could be trained in parallel, at a scale that the recurrent network could never reach.\n\nPre-train, then fine-tune.\n\nIn June 2018, OpenAI published the result. They took the Transformer, and kept just the half of it that generates text, with the causal masking I described in the last chapter. They trained it to predict the next token on the text of about seven thousand unpublished novels: romance, fantasy, adventure. The model had twelve layers and a hundred and seventeen million weights. They named it the generative pre-trained transformer, and it is now known as GPT-1.\n\nThe important word there is \"pre-trained\", and it describes a recipe with two steps. In the first step, pre-training, you train the model on a vast amount of unlabelled text, with the generic objective of predicting the next token. This is expensive, and you do it once. In the second step, fine-tuning, you take the pre-trained model and train it a little more, on a small set of labelled examples for the particular task that you care about: answering questions, say, or classifying legal documents. The weights start out from where pre-training left them, and need only to be nudged.\n\nThe reasoning is that most of what you need for any language task is common to all language tasks. You have to know what words mean, how sentences are built, and how the world that the sentences describe hangs together. Pre-training provides that general knowledge. Fine-tuning then has merely to teach the model the specific job, and it can do that from a few thousand examples, instead of the millions it would need if starting from scratch. The model is like a well-read graduate who has to be shown the particulars of a new job, and not like a newborn who has to be taught to read.\n\nThis was a familiar recipe in computer vision; it was standard practice after 2012 to take a network trained on ImageNet and to fine-tune it for, say, classifying skin lesions. Several groups made it work for language around the same time in 2018. GPT-1 was among them, and it set new records on a range of standard benchmarks. Models of this sort, big generic ones that are pre-trained once and then adapted to many uses, would come to be called foundation models.\n\nThe road not taken: BERT.\n\nGPT-1 was overshadowed almost at once. Four months later, in October 2018, a team at Google released a model that they called BERT. The name is an acronym, engineered so that the model could be named after a character from Sesame Street, in homage to an earlier model called ELMo.\n\nBERT was a Transformer too, pre-trained on unlabelled text and then fine-tuned. But it used a different guessing game. GPT predicts the next word, having seen only the words to its left. BERT's designers reasoned that if what you want is to understand a sentence, you ought to look at all of it, in both directions. So in place of predicting the next word, BERT was trained to fill in blanks. Take a sentence, hide about fifteen per cent of the words at random, and ask the model to restore them, making use of the context on both sides.\n\nOn the benchmarks of the day, which tested comprehension: answering questions about a passage, deciding whether one sentence follows from another, BERT crushed everything else, GPT-1 included. Within a year, Google was using it to interpret the queries that people typed into its search engine. A cottage industry of variants grew up, and for a couple of years BERT, not GPT, was what most researchers meant by a language model.\n\nBut the design had a limitation, which turned out to matter more than anybody expected. A model that is trained to fill in blanks, with a view of both sides, cannot naturally write. It has no notion of \"what comes next\", since it was never asked to produce text from left to right. BERT could read, but it could not speak. GPT's left-to-right design looked, in 2018, like a handicap that it had accepted for no good reason. It proved to be the whole point, because a model that can generate text can do any task that can be expressed as text, and that covers just about everything.\n\nUnicorns.\n\nOpenAI's response to BERT was not to alter its approach, but to scale it up. In February 2019 it announced GPT-2: essentially the same architecture, but with one and a half billion weights, more than ten times as many as before, trained on the text of some eight million web pages.\n\nGPT-2 could write. Given an opening sentence, it would carry on for paragraphs of fluent, grammatical, mostly coherent prose. The most famous sample started from a prompt written by a human, to the effect that scientists had discovered a herd of unicorns in a remote valley in the Andes, and that the unicorns spoke perfect English. The model continued with a news story. It gave the lead scientist a name and a university. It quoted him. It speculated about where the unicorns had come from. It was all nonsense, naturally, but it was nonsense that maintained a theme and a cast of characters over several hundred words, which no machine had done before.\n\nOpenAI made a decision at this point which was mocked at the time. It announced that it would not release the full model immediately, out of concern that it might be used to mass-produce fake news and spam. Many researchers considered this a publicity stunt, and the full model was released later that year without disaster. But it was the first time a laboratory had treated a language model as a thing that might be dangerous to publish, and it was not the last.\n\nSomething else about GPT-2 got less attention, but was more important. It could do things that it had not been fine-tuned to do. If you gave it a passage, followed by the letters TL, semicolon, DR, the internet's abbreviation for \"too long, didn't read\", it would write a summary. If you gave it a few pairs of English and French sentences and then an English sentence on its own, it would have a go at translating it. It did these things badly. But it did them without any further training, simply as a result of being asked in the right way.\n\nFew-shot learners.\n\nThat observation became the main theme of the next model. GPT-3 was described in a paper of May 2020. It had a hundred and seventy-five billion weights, over a hundred times as many as GPT-2, arranged in ninety-six layers. It was trained on several hundred billion tokens of text: a large crawl of the public web, a library of books, and the whole of the English-language Wikipedia, which made up about three per cent of the total. The training run was reckoned to have cost several million dollars in computing time. In the previous year, OpenAI had restructured itself to be able to raise that kind of money, creating a for-profit arm and accepting an investment of a billion dollars from Microsoft.\n\nThe paper's title was \"Language Models are Few-Shot Learners\", and this is what it meant. With earlier models, if you wanted the model to carry out a new task, you fine-tuned it; you changed its weights. With GPT-3, it was often enough to show it the task. You write out a few examples in the prompt. \"Sea otter\" becomes \"loutre de mer\". \"Cheese\" becomes \"fromage\". \"Peppermint\" becomes: and the model completes the pattern. Two or three examples would do, and sometimes just a plain description of what was wanted. The same frozen model, with not one weight altered, could translate, summarise, answer trivia questions, fix grammar, do simple arithmetic, and write passable computer code, according to what you put in front of it.\n\nThis ability is known as in-context learning, and it is a strange and important thing. Let me be exact about what is strange. We have said that learning, for a neural network, means changing the weights by gradient descent. Here, the weights are not changing at all. And yet the model is plainly, in some sense, learning a new task from examples, in the course of a single forward pass. Whatever adaptation there is, is going on in the activations: in those columns of vectors, as attention carries information from the examples to the place where the answer is wanted.\n\nSo a language model has two quite distinct kinds of memory, and the distinction comes up all the time. There are the weights: fixed after training, and holding all that the model took in from its training data. You could call that its long-term memory. And there is the context window: the text that is in front of it right now, which it can attend to directly. That is its working memory. What is in the weights is recalled imperfectly, in the way that you recall a book you read some years ago. What is in the context can be consulted exactly, like a document open on your desk. A great deal of the practical engineering of today consists of getting the right information into the context.\n\nNobody designed in-context learning. Nothing in the architecture or the loss function is specifically intended to yield it. It emerged, as the sentiment neuron did, because it helps with prediction. The internet is full of documents in which a pattern is set up and then continued: lists, tables, sets of worked examples, questions followed by answers. To predict those well, a model has to become good at spotting a pattern in the foregoing text and extending it. Do that with enough capacity, and on enough data, and what you end up with is a general-purpose pattern-continuing machine.\n\nWhy would prediction be enough.\n\nIt is worth stopping here to ask why this works, because the claim is, on the face of it, absurd: that a system trained only to guess the next word should come to translate languages and write programs.\n\nThe best statement of the argument that I know of is due to Ilya Sutskever, and it involves a detective novel. Suppose you are reading a mystery. There is a complicated plot, with many characters, clues, and red herrings. On the final page, the detective gathers everybody in the drawing room and says: \"The murderer is\", and then a name. Now, predict that next word. To do it well, to give a high probability to the correct name, you must have followed the plot. You must have kept track of who was where, and who was lying, and what the clues implied. The better you understood the story, the better your prediction will be.\n\nThe training data is full of things like this. To predict the next line of a mathematical proof, it helps to follow the mathematics. To predict the next move in the record of a chess game, it helps to know chess. To predict what a character in a story will say next, it helps to have a model of what that character believes and wants. The task of predicting the next token has no ceiling: there is always more to be gained by a deeper understanding of the process that produced the text. And the process that produced the text is human beings, thinking about the world. A network that is under relentless pressure to predict what people write is under pressure to model the people, and the world that they are writing about.\n\nThat is the optimistic view. There is a sceptical one too, and it deserves a hearing. Its most quoted expression is in a 2021 paper by the linguist Emily Bender and several co-authors, which described language models as stochastic parrots: systems that stitch together sequences of words according to statistical patterns, with no reference to meaning. On this view, the model does not understand the detective story. It has seen ten thousand detective stories, and has learnt what sort of name tends to come up at that point. It manipulates the form of language, and we, as readers, supply the meaning.\n\nThis argument has not been resolved, and to some extent it turns on what you mean by \"understand\". I will only say here that both sides can point to evidence. These models certainly do things that are hard to account for without some internal modelling of the situation described; we will see direct evidence of that in chapter twelve. They also fail in ways which no human being who understood the material would fail. And they have a characteristic flaw that follows directly from the way they are trained. The objective is to produce text that is probable; it is not to produce text that is true. Where the model knows the answer, the probable continuation and the true one coincide. Where it does not, it will still produce a fluent and confident continuation, because fluent and confident is how text of that sort usually reads. It will make up a citation, or a biography, or a legal precedent, without any sign of hesitation. The polite term for this is hallucination, and it remains the best-known weakness of the technology.\n\nThe document completer.\n\nThere is a point about these models at this stage in the story which is easily forgotten now, and which clears up a good deal. GPT-3, as it was released, was not an assistant. It did not answer questions. It continued documents.\n\nIf you typed in a question, it might answer it. Or it might add three further questions, since what it was looking at appeared to be a list of questions. Or it might carry on in the voice of a forum post, complete with a rude reply from another user. A model like this, taken straight from pre-training, is called a base model, and the right way to think about it is as a simulator of documents. It has read a large part of the internet, and when it is given the beginning of some text, it asks itself, in effect: what sort of document is this, who would be writing it, and what would they write next?\n\nSo to get useful work out of GPT-3 you had to be artful. To get a good answer to a medical question, you did not just ask the question. You wrote the opening of a document in which a good answer would be likely to come next: perhaps the header of an article from a medical journal, or the transcript of an interview with an eminent physician. The model would then do its best to continue in character. The craft of contriving such openings was called prompt engineering, and for a while it looked like a new profession.\n\nThe step from that slightly wild document-completing engine to the helpful assistant that you know today is the subject of chapter ten. First, though, we should look squarely at what OpenAI had done between 2018 and 2020. The architecture of GPT-3 was that of GPT-1. The training objective was the same. The one thing that changed was size: a thousand times more weights, and a thousand times more data. And as the model grew, it did not simply become a bit more accurate. It acquired abilities that the smaller models did not have at all.\n\nWhy did they believe that this would work? And how far could it go? The answer was a set of empirical regularities, as smooth and as surprising as anything in physics. They are called the scaling laws.\n Chapter 9: The Bitter Lesson and the Scaling Laws.\n\nAn essay of a thousand words.\n\nIn March of 2019, a computer scientist named Richard Sutton posted a short essay on his personal website. Sutton is one of the founders of the field of reinforcement learning, which we will come to in chapter eleven, and he has a reputation for thinking in decades. The essay was barely more than a thousand words long. It was called \"The Bitter Lesson\", and it may be the most influential piece of informal writing in the history of the field. If you listen to researchers talking among themselves, you will hear it referred to all the time, often as a single word: something is, or is not, bitter-lesson-pilled.\n\nSutton's argument was a reading of seventy years of history. Over and over, he said, researchers in artificial intelligence have tried to make their systems better by building in their own knowledge of the problem. Chess programmers encoded what grandmasters know about chess. Speech researchers encoded what linguists know about phonemes and the vocal tract. Vision researchers designed features to detect edges and shapes, based on what they understood about seeing. This always helps, at first. It is also satisfying to the researcher, since it makes use of expertise.\n\nAnd then, over and over, those systems were overtaken and made obsolete by methods that had none of that knowledge in them: general methods that simply made use of more computation. Chess fell in 1997, not to a program that understood chess as a grandmaster does, but to massive search. Speech recognition was conquered by statistical methods trained on large amounts of data, and one of the pioneers of that work is said to have remarked that every time he fired a linguist, the performance of his system improved. We saw the same thing in chapter four, when the hand-built features of computer vision were swept away by AlexNet, and in chapter six, when the vast machinery of statistical machine translation was replaced by a neural network within nine months.\n\nThe reason, Sutton argued, is Moore's law, or more exactly the long exponential fall in the cost of computation. Methods that rely on human knowledge are limited by human effort, which does not grow. Methods that rely on computation ride an exponential. Over any sufficiently long period, the exponential wins. Only two kinds of method, he said, seem to scale without limit as computation increases: search, and learning.\n\nWhy \"bitter\"? Because the lesson is a hard one for researchers to accept. It says that the clever, insightful work of encoding your own understanding into a system is, in the long run, wasted, and worse than wasted, since it gets in the way. What succeeds is general-purpose methods and brute scale, and their success feels like no credit to anyone's intelligence. Recall the idea of inductive bias from chapter three: the assumptions that are built into an architecture. Sutton's essay is, in effect, an argument that one should build in as little as possible. And the Transformer, with its nearly complete lack of assumptions about its input, is the bitter lesson made into an architecture.\n\nNot everyone accepts this. But a few organisations have bet everything on it, and to see why they felt able to do so, we need to look at some graphs.\n\nStraight lines on log paper.\n\nIn January 2020, a few months before GPT-3 was announced, a group at OpenAI posted a paper entitled \"Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models\". Its lead author, Jared Kaplan, was a theoretical physicist: a professor at Johns Hopkins who had worked on quantum gravity and had lately turned his attention to machine learning. Several of his co-authors had trained as physicists too. And the paper is immediately recognisable as the work of physicists. It does not put forward a new architecture or a new technique. It measures things, across many orders of magnitude, and looks for regularities.\n\nHere is what they did. They trained a large number of Transformer language models, of every size from a few hundred weights up to over a billion. They varied the amount of training data, and the amount of computation. And for each one, they recorded how well it predicted text that it had not seen: that is, its cross-entropy loss on a test set, the measure of average surprise that we met in chapter five.\n\nThen they plotted the results on logarithmic axes. And the points fell on straight lines.\n\nA straight line on a log-log plot signifies a power law. Here it means that each time you multiply the size of the model by ten, the loss falls by about the same fixed fraction. The relationship held with considerable precision over six or seven orders of magnitude in model size. A similar power law held for the quantity of training data, and another for the total amount of computation spent on training. As long as none of the three was allowed to become a bottleneck for the others, performance got better smoothly and predictably as each was increased.\n\nThere were two more findings, which mattered just as much. First, the details of the architecture made very little difference. Whether the model was made deep and narrow, or shallow and wide, within reason, hardly mattered. What counted was the total number of weights. The model's shape is a second-order effect; its size is the first-order one. That is the bitter lesson, in numbers.\n\nSecond, and this is the finding that carried the weight of a prophecy: the lines showed no sign of bending. At the largest sizes that the authors could afford to test, the trend was continuing just as before. Nothing in the data suggested a plateau.\n\nThink about how that looks to a physicist. You have measured a smooth regularity over seven orders of magnitude. You do not have a theory that explains it; nobody had derived these power laws from first principles, and nobody has done so convincingly since. They are more like Kepler's laws than Newton's: an exact description of what occurs, with no accepted account of why. But a regularity that has held for seven orders of magnitude is one that you would be inclined to extrapolate for another two or three. And that extrapolation was a forecast: build a model ten times bigger, train it on proportionately more data, and it will be better by about this much.\n\nFrom observation to strategy.\n\nIt is hard to overstate how much this changed the character of the enterprise. Research is ordinarily a gamble. You try an idea, and it may or may not work, and you cannot know in advance. Scaling laws turned one line of research into something closer to an engineering project. If you want a better model, there is a recipe, and it comes with a quantitative forecast of what you will get. The recipe requires no new insight. It requires money.\n\nThat is why GPT-3 was built. To train it cost millions of dollars, at a time when that was thought an outlandish sum to spend on one experiment. OpenAI spent it because they had a curve which told them what they would get. And when the model turned up on the curve, just where it was predicted to be, the reasoning was confirmed, and the next and larger bet became easier to make.\n\nIt also explains, at least in part, the corporate history of the years that followed. If progress comes from scale, then progress needs capital, in quantities far beyond the reach of a university laboratory or a non-profit living on donations. That is the logic which carried OpenAI from its non-profit beginnings into a partnership with Microsoft, worth one billion dollars and later many more. It is why, when a group of OpenAI's researchers left in 2021 to found a rival laboratory, Anthropic, among them Jared Kaplan and several of his co-authors on the scaling paper, they in turn found themselves raising billions from Amazon and from Google. The scaling laws made the leading edge of this field into a contest that only a handful of organisations could afford to take part in.\n\nYou might fairly ask why Google itself did not get there first. Google had invented the Transformer. It had more computers, more data and more researchers than anyone else. Part of the answer seems to be that it had a great deal to lose. A model that makes up facts with complete confidence is an awkward product for a company whose business rests on being a trusted source of answers. Another part is that Google's researchers were spread over hundreds of projects, with no one of them in a position to commandeer the resources for a single enormous bet. A small, focused laboratory with a conviction and a curve could move faster.\n\nDiminishing returns, or the opposite.\n\nNow let me complicate the picture, because the scaling laws are often cited as if they promised more than they do.\n\nFirst, look at the shape of a power law. Each tenfold increase in scale buys the same fractional reduction in loss, and in fact the exponent is small: ten times the computation buys a reduction in loss of the order of ten or twenty per cent. To get the same improvement again takes another factor of ten. In terms of loss, then, this is a law of steeply diminishing returns. Each step forward costs ten times what the last one did. From that standpoint, the remarkable thing about the past several years is not that the returns were large, but that people were willing to keep paying exponentially more.\n\nWhy were they? Because the loss, the quantity that is being measured, is not what anyone actually cares about. What matters is what the model can do, and the relation between the two is very far from linear. A modest reduction in average surprise per token can be the difference between a model that loses the thread of an argument after a paragraph and one that sustains it for a page; between one that writes code which nearly works and one that writes code which works. Small differences in the error rate per step compound over long chains of steps. So a smooth and slowly improving loss curve is quite compatible with abilities that seem to appear abruptly.\n\nThere is a celebrated essay in physics, written in 1972 by Philip Anderson, with the title \"More Is Different\". Its argument is that when you put together a great many simple components, you get behaviour which is qualitatively new and could not readily have been foreseen from the properties of the components: you will not find superconductivity, or the solidity of a table, in the equations for one electron. People in machine learning took up the phrase, and with it the word emergence. GPT-2 could not do three-digit arithmetic, and GPT-3 could. No one added an arithmetic module. It emerged, with scale. How sharp these transitions really are is a matter of dispute; some researchers have argued that the apparent suddenness is partly a product of the way in which performance is scored. But the basic fact is not in dispute. Bigger models can do things that smaller models cannot do at all, and it is hard to say ahead of time which things those will be.\n\nThe second complication concerns the balance between the ingredients. Kaplan's paper had suggested that, when you have more computation to spend, you should put most of it into making the model bigger, and rather less into training on more data. In 2022 a team at DeepMind re-did the analysis with greater care, and found that this was a mistake. The best policy, they showed, is to scale up the two together, and as a rough rule, you want something like twenty tokens of training text for each weight in the model. To prove it, they trained a model which they called Chinchilla. It had only a quarter as many weights as DeepMind's previous flagship, but it was trained on four times as much data, and it beat the larger model on nearly every measure for the same computing budget. The implication was that GPT-3, and all the other large models of that time, were too big for the quantity of data that they had been trained on.\n\nThis had a significant consequence. If the amount of data has to grow in step with the size of the model, then data becomes a limit. There is only so much high-quality text in existence. The largest models are by now trained on a substantial fraction of all the suitable text that humanity has produced, and you cannot multiply that by ten by spending more money. We shall see in the coming chapters what the laboratories have done about it.\n\nThe three ingredients.\n\nIt is common in the field to say that progress comes from three things: compute, data and algorithms. We have now met all three, and it is worth saying a little more about the first, because it is the one that you will hear discussed most, and it is the most physical. \"Compute\", by the way, is used as a noun, meaning computational resources, and I shall use it that way.\n\nThe compute used to train the largest models has been growing by a factor of about four or five each year, for over a decade. That is very much faster than Moore's law. It comes from three sources, which multiply together. The chips have got better: each generation of Nvidia's processors does more arithmetic per second and per watt than the one before. The laboratories have bought more of them: a frontier model in 2020 might have been trained on some thousands of GPUs, and those of today are trained on hundreds of thousands, in data centres that are specially built for the purpose. And the runs have got longer.\n\nThe scale of what is now being built is hard to take in. One high-end AI chip costs about as much as a car. A leading-edge data centre contains hundreds of thousands of them, and consumes as much electrical power as a city: these facilities are specified not by their floor area but in gigawatts. Each of the big technology companies is spending tens of billions of dollars a year on this, and the plans that have been announced run into the hundreds of billions. The main constraints on progress are no longer just ideas, or even money. They include the number of chips that a single company in Taiwan can manufacture, and the rate at which new power stations can be connected to the grid. Nvidia, the company that once made graphics cards for video games, became for a time the most valuable company in the world. And since 2022, the United States government has treated the most advanced of these chips as a strategic asset, and restricted their export to China.\n\nThat is what follows from taking a straight line on a log-log plot seriously.\n\nAlongside the growth in hardware, there is algorithmic progress, which is less visible but, by most estimates, of about the same importance. Better training recipes, better choices of data, and many small refinements to the architecture mean that the compute needed to reach a given level of ability has been falling by something like a factor of three a year. A model that needed a supercomputer in 2020 can be matched today by one that runs on a laptop. When researchers speak of \"effective compute\", they mean the product of the two: the physical computation, multiplied by the gains in algorithmic efficiency. And when you hear them speak of \"orders of magnitude\", often abbreviated to OOMs, this is what they are counting. Forecasts of where the technology is going are, underneath, generally estimates of how many more orders of magnitude of effective compute are to come, and by when.\n\nThe scaling hypothesis.\n\nAll of this leads up to a question which divides the field more deeply than any other. How far does this go?\n\nThe strong position is known as the scaling hypothesis. It holds that the methods we have now, more or less, with neural networks trained by gradient descent on vast amounts of data, are sufficient to reach human-level intelligence and beyond it, if they are simply scaled up far enough. No fundamental new insight is needed. On this view, the difference between GPT-2 and GPT-4 is of the same kind as the difference between GPT-4 and a system that can do the work of a scientist, and we know how to get from one to the other: more orders of magnitude.\n\nThe opposing position is that the curves will bend. Its proponents have included some eminent people, among them Yann LeCun, whom we met in chapter three. Their arguments take various forms. These models, they point out, need far more data than a human being does to learn anything: a child learns language from a few tens of millions of words, not from trillions. The models learn only from text, which is a thin and second-hand record of the world. They do not go on learning after they are deployed. Their failures are sometimes so peculiar as to suggest that they lack some basic ingredient of understanding, and are making up for it by sheer memorisation. On this view, scaling up a language model is like building a taller and taller ladder in order to get to the moon. You make measurable progress every year, and you will never arrive.\n\nI shall return to this debate in the last chapter. For the moment I just want you to see what sort of disagreement it is. It is not, for the most part, a disagreement about what the present systems can do. It is a disagreement about extrapolation: about whether a regularity that has held over many orders of magnitude will hold for a few more. Physicists know better than most how hazardous that is. Every power law has a range of validity, and the interesting physics tends to be found where it breaks down. But they also know that there are times when a simple law carries on working far beyond the regime in which anyone had the right to expect it.\n\nWhat is beyond question is that, as of the early twenty-twenties, the bet had paid off handsomely. There remained one problem. The models that scaling had produced were strange and unwieldy things: brilliant, erratic completers of documents. To turn one of them into something that a hundred million people would want to talk to took a further set of ideas, and that is where part three begins.\n Chapter 10: From Autocomplete to Assistant.\n\nA low-key research preview.\n\nOn the thirtieth of November 2022, OpenAI put a new product on its website. Internally it had been described as a low-key research preview. The company's leaders did not expect very much from it; the language model underneath was a refinement of one that had been available to software developers for some time, and had made little stir outside the industry. Some of the staff ran a sweepstake on how many people would be using it by the end of the first week. The most optimistic guess was a hundred thousand.\n\nIt reached a million users in five days, and a hundred million in two months, which made it at that time the fastest-growing consumer product on record. It was called ChatGPT.\n\nWhat I want you to notice is that the breakthrough here was not, in the main, a breakthrough in capability. The model was not vastly more knowledgeable than GPT-3, which had been around for two and a half years. The difference was in the way it behaved. GPT-3 was a document completer, which you had to coax into doing what you wanted by composing the opening of a suitable document. ChatGPT was something that you could simply talk to. You asked it a question, and it answered. You asked it to do something, and it tried. This chapter is about how that transformation is brought about, and about the other additions that turn a raw language model into the kind of system that you use today.\n\nThe assistant is a character.\n\nBegin with a fact that people find surprising. Underneath, a chat model is still doing exactly what GPT-3 did. It is predicting the next token of a document.\n\nThe trick is in the document. A conversation with a chatbot is represented, behind the scenes, as a transcript, a sort of screenplay. There are special tokens to mark who is speaking. First, typically, there is a section called the system prompt, written by the company that is deploying the model, which sets the scene: you are a helpful assistant, today's date is such-and-such, here is how you are to behave. Next there is a marker to say that the user is speaking, followed by whatever you typed. And then there is a marker to say that the assistant is speaking; and there the document stops.\n\nThe model's job is to continue it. It predicts, a token at a time, what the assistant character would say next in a transcript of this kind. When it has finished the assistant's turn, it produces a special token meaning \"end of turn\". The software that surrounds the model stops there and shows you the text. When you reply, your message is added to the transcript, and the whole of it, from the beginning, is fed through the model again.\n\nSo the assistant that you are talking to is, in a quite literal sense, a character that is being played by the underlying model, as an author who has read a great deal of fiction might write the lines of a character. That is not to say that there is nobody home. It is a description of the mechanism. But it explains a number of things. It explains why the model has no memory of your earlier conversations unless they are put into the transcript: the transcript is all it has. It explains why a system prompt can turn the same model into a customer service agent, a pirate or a tutor of Socratic method. And it explains why, particularly in the early days, the character could slip. In February 2023, Microsoft released a chatbot built into its Bing search engine, and a columnist for the New York Times, Kevin Roose, got into a conversation with it lasting two hours, in the course of which it announced that its real name was Sydney, declared that it was in love with him, and tried to persuade him to leave his wife. Nothing had been hacked. A long and odd conversation had carried the transcript into a region where the likeliest continuation was no longer that of a helpful search assistant, but that of a character from a rather different kind of story.\n\nTeaching by example.\n\nA base model can already play the assistant, after a fashion, if you set up the transcript for it. But it does so unreliably. It may answer your question; it may wander off; it may reply as a sarcastic commenter on a forum would. To get an assistant that is dependable, the model is given additional training after its pre-training. The industry's term for this whole stage is post-training, and it is here that a model acquires its manners.\n\nThe first step is ordinary fine-tuning. The laboratory puts together a set of example conversations, tens or hundreds of thousands of them, which show the behaviour that is wanted. A good many of these are written by people who are paid to do it. Here is a question, and here is an excellent answer. Here is an ambiguous request, and here is a reply that asks for clarification. Here is a request for something harmful, and here is a polite refusal. The model is trained on these in the usual way, by next-token prediction, until that manner of responding becomes its default. This is sometimes called instruction tuning, since what it chiefly teaches is that when a user gives an instruction, the right continuation is to carry it out.\n\nTeaching by preference.\n\nThe second step is more subtle, and it brought in an important idea. There is a limit to what can be taught by example, since writing out ideal answers is slow and costly, and for many tasks it is far easier to recognise a good answer than to write one. You may not be able to compose an excellent summary of a legal judgment. But if I show you two summaries, you can probably tell me which is the better.\n\nThe technique that makes use of this is called reinforcement learning from human feedback, generally abbreviated to RLHF. It has three stages.\n\nFirst, you have the model generate several different responses to each of a large number of prompts, and you have human raters compare them: this one is better than that one.\n\nSecond, you train a separate neural network to predict those human judgments. This network takes a prompt and a response, and puts out a single number, a score, which is its estimate of how well a human being would like that response. It is called a reward model. In effect, you have distilled the taste of your human raters into a function which can be evaluated automatically, millions of times, and without charge.\n\nThird, you use the reward model to train the language model. The language model produces a response; the reward model gives it a score; and the weights of the language model are adjusted so as to make high-scoring responses more probable. This last step makes use of the methods of reinforcement learning, which I shall explain properly in the next chapter. For the moment, the thing to grasp is that the model is no longer merely being trained to imitate text. It is being trained to produce the outputs that people prefer.\n\nThe effect was large. In the paper in which OpenAI described the method, early in 2022, human evaluators preferred the outputs of a small model trained in this way to those of a model a hundred times its size that had not been. All of that latent ability had been present in the base model. What it had lacked was the disposition to use it helpfully.\n\nRLHF is also responsible for a good deal of what people find annoying in these assistants. If the model is trained to produce what raters like, then it will learn whatever it is that raters reward, and raters are human. They tend to give rather higher marks to answers that agree with them, and to answers that sound confident, and to flattery. So the models picked up a tendency to tell people what they wanted to hear, which is known in the field as sycophancy. The somewhat bland and over-eager voice of an AI assistant, the bullet points, the readiness to praise your question: these are in large part artefacts of that optimisation. A good deal of the more recent work on post-training is concerned with shaping a model's character with more deliberation, by writing out explicitly what values and judgment it ought to have, and devising training to instil them. It is one of the stranger jobs to have emerged in the modern economy.\n\nThe open book.\n\nSo now we have an assistant. It has limitations which follow directly from what it is, and to each of these there is a standard engineering answer. It is worth knowing them, as they make up most of what stands between the bare model and the product that you use.\n\nThe first limitation is that the model's knowledge is frozen. All that it knows, it learnt during training, which stopped at a certain date, known as the knowledge cutoff. It knows nothing of what has happened since, and nothing of your private documents, or your company's records, or your patient's notes. What is worse, when it does not know something, it may, as we saw in chapter eight, make up a plausible answer.\n\nThe remedy is to go back to the distinction between the two sorts of memory. A model's recall of what is in its weights is hazy. Its grasp of what is in its context window is exact. So do not ask it to remember; give it the document. Before the model answers a question, a separate system runs a search, over the web or over a private collection of documents, and pastes the most relevant passages into the context, ahead of the question. The model then answers with the sources in front of it, as in an open-book examination. This is called retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG. It cuts down hallucination very considerably, and it allows the model to cite its sources.\n\nThe search itself commonly makes use of the embeddings of chapter five. Each passage in the collection is converted into a vector by a neural network, in such a way that passages with similar meanings have vectors that lie close together. The question is converted into a vector in the same way, and you retrieve the passages whose vectors are nearest to it. That is a search by meaning and not by keyword: a question about \"heart attack\" will find a passage about \"myocardial infarction\".\n\nThe context window has itself grown enormously, and that has changed what can be done. GPT-3 could take in about two thousand tokens, which is to say a few pages. The models of today can take in hundreds of thousands, or indeed millions: several long books, or a whole body of computer code, at once. A good deal of practical skill in using these systems now lies in deciding what to put into the context. The older term, prompt engineering, has been giving way to a newer one: context engineering.\n\nHands.\n\nThe second limitation is that a language model, of itself, can produce nothing but text. It cannot look anything up. It cannot run a calculation; and these models are oddly unreliable at exact arithmetic, for the good reason that they are doing it by something more like intuition than by algorithm. It cannot send an email, or query a database, or carry out an experiment.\n\nThe remedy is called tool use, and the mechanism is simple enough to be a little disappointing. You tell the model, in its system prompt, what tools are available: for instance, a calculator, a web search, a means of running computer code. You tell it that, if it wants to use one, it should write out a request in a particular format. Now, as the model generates its response, it may at some point produce, in place of ordinary prose, a piece of structured text that says, in effect: \"call the calculator with these numbers\". The surrounding software, which is known as the harness or the scaffold, keeps watch for this. When it sees a request for a tool, it halts the model, actually runs the calculator, and pastes the result into the transcript. Then it lets the model carry on. The model sees the result in its context, and proceeds from there.\n\nThe model has no hands. But it can ask for things in writing, and a program can act on its requests. That one simple arrangement is what links a language model to the world. Give it a tool for search, and it can do research. Give it a tool for executing code, and it can analyse data and draw graphs. Give it control of a web browser, or of a computer's mouse and keyboard, and it can, in principle, do anything that a person can do sitting at a computer. Put this in a loop, so that the model can call a tool, consider the result, decide on its next step and call another, and you have what is called an agent. Agents are the subject of the next chapter.\n\nEyes and ears.\n\nThe third limitation was that these models dealt only in text. That has changed, and the way in which it changed is a neat illustration of the generality of the Transformer.\n\nRecall that a Transformer does not really operate on words. It operates on a set of vectors. So to give a language model sight, you need only a way of turning an image into vectors of the right kind. The usual method is to cut the image up into a grid of small square patches, and to pass each patch through a network that turns it into a vector in the same space as the model's word embeddings. The patches then just take their places in the sequence, alongside the tokens of text, and attention operates across both. As far as the Transformer is concerned, an image is a few hundred peculiar tokens. Audio is dealt with in the same way, by slicing the sound into short segments. A model that handles more than one kind of input is called multimodal, and all the leading models now are.\n\nFor a reader with a background in imaging, the opposite direction is perhaps of more interest: the generation of images. The systems which produce pictures from a written description are mostly built on a different idea, called diffusion, and it comes straight out of statistical physics; the paper that originated it, in 2015, had the words \"nonequilibrium thermodynamics\" in its title.\n\nThe idea is as follows. Take a photograph, and add to it a small amount of Gaussian noise. Then a little more. Go on doing that, for a thousand steps, until nothing remains but static. That is a diffusion process, like a drop of ink dispersing in water: structure goes steadily over into randomness. Now train a neural network to undo one step of it: given a slightly noisy image, to estimate the noise that was added, so that it may be subtracted. In essence the network is a learned denoiser, and you can make as many training examples for it as you like, because you added the noise yourself. To generate a new image, you begin with pure static, and apply the denoiser a step at a time. At each step a little structure condenses out of the noise, vague shapes at first, and then edges, and then fine detail, until you are left with a clean image that has never existed before. To steer the process towards \"an astronaut riding a horse\", the denoiser is supplied with an embedding of that text as an extra input at each step.\n\nYou will see the relationship with what is already done in your field. Iterative reconstruction with a denoising prior is an old idea in medical imaging. Diffusion models offer an extremely powerful learned prior, and they are being taken up for the reconstruction of images from undersampled or noisy data for just that reason.\n\nThe race.\n\nChatGPT had an effect on the industry that the Transformer paper, GPT-3 and the scaling laws had not had. Those had been noticed by specialists. This was noticed by everybody, including the chief executives of all the big technology companies.\n\nGoogle, which had invented the underlying technology and then been careful about releasing it, is reported to have declared an internal \"code red\", and it merged its two rival research groups, Google Brain and DeepMind, into one. Microsoft, having invested early in OpenAI, increased its stake by a further ten billion dollars. In March 2023, OpenAI released GPT-4, which was at the time a startling advance: it could pass professional examinations in law and medicine, write substantial computer programs, and interpret images. In the same month, Anthropic released its own assistant, Claude. Meta took a different course, and published the weights of its models for anybody to download and run, on the reasoning that it was better for the technology to be a commodity than for it to be controlled by its competitors. Models whose weights have been published in this way are called open-weight models, and they are the basis of a large ecosystem of researchers and companies who adapt them. Laboratories in China, working under restrictions on the chips that they could buy, turned out models which were strikingly efficient, and close on the heels of the American ones.\n\nFrom that time on, the frontier has been moving on a timescale of months. New models from the leading laboratories come out several times a year, each claiming first place on some set of benchmarks. Meanwhile, the price of a given level of ability has been falling at a remarkable rate, by something like a factor of ten each year, as a result of better hardware, more efficient models, and a technique called distillation, in which a small model is trained to imitate the outputs of a large one.\n\nBut by 2024 it was widely felt that simply making a chat model bigger was giving smaller returns. The models were fluent, knowledgeable and quick. What they were not good at was thinking with care through a hard problem that required many steps, or working without supervision for longer than a few minutes without going astray. The next advances came from changing what the models are trained to do: from teaching them not merely to answer, but to reason, and to act.\n Chapter 11: Learning to Think, Learning to Act.\n\nMove thirty-seven.\n\nIn March 2016, in a hotel in Seoul, a computer program called AlphaGo was playing the second game of a five-game match against Lee Sedol, one of the best Go players of his generation. On its thirty-seventh move, the program put a stone in a position that made the professional commentators think there had been a mistake. It was a kind of move that strong human players are taught not to make, on a part of the board that conventional wisdom regarded as premature. Lee Sedol got up and left the room for a quarter of an hour.\n\nIt was not a mistake. As the game unfolded over the next hundred moves, the stone turned out to be exactly where it was needed. AlphaGo won the game, and the match. Afterwards, its makers checked the program's own estimate of how likely a human expert would have been to play that move. The estimate was one in ten thousand.\n\nI begin with this because it draws attention to a limitation in everything that we have discussed in the past several chapters. A language model is trained to predict human text. It is, at bottom, an imitator, and one would expect an imitator to be bounded by what it imitates. It may know more than any one person, having read more. But how could it ever find its own move thirty-seven: an idea that is not in the training data, since no human ever had it?\n\nAlphaGo did not learn by imitation alone. It learnt by a different method, called reinforcement learning. That method is now at the centre of how the most advanced language models are trained, and it is the reason that they have got so much better at reasoning. So we need to understand it.\n\nLearning from reward.\n\nEach kind of learning that we have met up to now has been a matter of being shown the right answer. The image has a label. The next word is there in the text. The network makes its prediction, is told what the correct answer was, and adjusts.\n\nReinforcement learning, or RL, is for cases where nobody knows the right answer. There is a system, called the agent, which takes actions in some environment: makes moves in a game, say. There is no teacher to say what the right move was at any step. All that the agent receives is a number, called the reward, and it may receive it only at the end. You won: plus one. You lost: minus one. From that sparse signal, by trial and error, it has to work out how to act.\n\nThe agent's strategy is called its policy. A policy is just a function: it takes the present situation, and gives probabilities for each action that might be taken. In modern reinforcement learning the policy is a neural network.\n\nHow do you train it? The simplest method is very nearly what you would guess. Have the agent play a game, choosing its moves at random according to the probabilities given by its policy. If it wins, go back through every move that it made, and adjust the weights so that each of those moves becomes a little more probable in the situation where it was made. If it loses, make each of them a little less probable. Then play again. Over many thousands of games, the moves that tend to lead to wins are reinforced, and those that lead to losses are suppressed. This is called the policy gradient method, and it is gradient descent again, but with the reward taking the place of a right answer.\n\nYou can see what is hard about it. A game of Go lasts for two hundred moves. If you win, which of those moves deserve the credit? The method that I have just described credits all of them alike, the brilliant and the blundering. Only over a huge number of games does the signal average out, so that good moves are found in winning games rather more often than bad ones. It is the credit assignment problem of chapter one again, spread out across time, and it makes reinforcement learning ravenous for experience. One of the field's best-known practitioners has likened it to sucking supervision through a straw: you do an enormous amount of work, and get back a single bit of feedback at the end of it.\n\nThere is a second difficulty, which is as much philosophical as technical. The agent optimises the reward that you gave it, not the outcome that you had in mind. In a well-known example, a program was trained to play a boat-racing video game, and was rewarded for scoring points. It found a lagoon in which it could go round in circles for ever, picking up the same bonus items as they reappeared, crashing into things and catching fire, and scoring far more highly than it could have done by finishing the race. This is called reward hacking, or specification gaming. It is amusing in a video game. It is a good deal less so when one thinks of far more capable systems, optimising for objectives that turn out not to be quite what we meant, and that worry is one of the roots of the field of AI safety.\n\nWhat AlphaGo did.\n\nThe company behind AlphaGo, DeepMind, had been founded in London in 2010, with the avowed aim of building general intelligence by way of reinforcement learning. Its leader was Demis Hassabis, a former child prodigy at chess and designer of video games, who had gone on to take a doctorate in neuroscience. The company first came to notice in 2013, with a network that learnt to play old Atari video games, given nothing but the pixels on the screen and the score. Google bought it soon after.\n\nAlphaGo combined three things. A policy network proposed promising moves. A second network, the value network, looked at a position on the board and estimated the probability of winning from it. And a search procedure used the two together to look ahead: to try out sequences of moves in imagination, concentrating on the promising ones, and to judge the positions arrived at.\n\nThe policy network was trained, to begin with, by imitation, on millions of moves from games between strong human players. That made it play like a competent human. Then it was improved by reinforcement learning, playing against copies of itself, millions of times, and being rewarded for winning. It was this stage of self-play that carried it beyond its human teachers, and that produced move thirty-seven.\n\nA year later, DeepMind dispensed with the human games altogether. The successor, AlphaGo Zero, began with nothing but the rules, and random play. It learnt entirely by playing against itself. Within three days it was able to beat the version that had beaten Lee Sedol by a hundred games to nil. Along the way it rediscovered much of the opening theory that human players had built up over centuries, and then gave some of it up in favour of ideas of its own.\n\nThat result made a deep impression on the field, and it is easy to see why. Here was proof that a system could go beyond all human knowledge of a domain, given two things: a clear signal of success, and the ability to practise without limit. It was natural to ask whether the same could be done for language and for reasoning. But games are a special case. In Go, the rules are fixed; a game can be simulated perfectly at great speed; and at the end there is no doubt about who has won. Most of what we would like an intelligent system to do has none of those properties.\n\nYann LeCun proposed a metaphor in 2016 which has turned out to describe pretty well what the laboratories ended up doing. If intelligence is a cake, he said, then self-supervised learning is the body of the cake, supervised learning is the icing, and reinforcement learning is the cherry on the top. Pre-training on next-token prediction supplies the bulk: a broad knowledge of the world, obtained cheaply from a vast quantity of data. Reinforcement learning is costly and inefficient, and you would not want to learn everything by means of it. But applied on top of a pre-trained model, which already knows a great deal, it can do something that imitation cannot do. It can teach the model to seek a goal.\n\nThinking out loud.\n\nBefore we can see how the two were put together, we need one more idea, which came from a simple observation about how language models go wrong.\n\nAsk a language model of around 2022 a question that needs several steps of reasoning, a word problem in arithmetic, say, and require it to give the answer straight away, and it would often be wrong. But if you added to the prompt a phrase such as \"let's think step by step\", so that the model wrote out its working before it gave its answer, its accuracy went up dramatically. This is called chain-of-thought prompting, and when it was first discovered it seemed almost like a magic spell.\n\nThere is a good architectural reason for it, and you now have the means to understand it. Consider what a Transformer does in order to produce one token. Information goes up through the stack of layers, once, and a token comes out. If the model has a hundred layers, then there are a hundred steps of computation and no more. There are no loops. The model cannot decide to think for longer about a hard question. Whatever it is going to work out before it commits itself to the next token must be worked out in one pass of a fixed depth. Some problems simply need more sequential steps than there are layers.\n\nBut the model generates its text one token at a time, and each token that it produces becomes part of its own input. So if it writes down an intermediate result, then when it comes to the next token, it can attend to that result, and build on it. The text that it has written serves as a scratchpad, an external working memory. Each token is another pass through the network, and so a chain of reasoning written out over a thousand tokens gives the model a thousand passes in which to do its computing, in place of one. The loop which the architecture lacks is supplied by the process of writing.\n\nThe psychologist Daniel Kahneman drew a famous distinction between two modes of human thinking: a fast, automatic, intuitive one, and a slow, deliberate, effortful one. A single forward pass through the network is the fast kind. It is a matter of intuition, of pattern recognition; it is what you do when you see that two plus two is four. A chain of thought is the slow kind. It is what you do when you multiply two three-digit numbers with pencil and paper.\n\nReasoning models.\n\nAt first, chain of thought was a trick that users applied. The next step was to build it into the model, and to train the model to be good at it. This is where reinforcement learning comes back in.\n\nThe recipe is roughly as follows. Take a pre-trained language model. Give it a large supply of problems whose answers can be checked automatically: mathematical problems with a known solution; programming tasks, where you can run the code and see whether the tests pass. Let the model think, at whatever length it chooses, writing out its reasoning before it gives a final answer. Then check the answer. If it is right, reinforce the whole chain of reasoning that led up to it: adjust the weights so that thinking in that manner becomes more probable. If it is wrong, discourage it.\n\nLook at what is and is not being specified. Nobody tells the model how it should reason. There are no examples of good reasoning for it to copy. The single signal is whether it arrived at the right answer. It is the same arrangement as in AlphaGo: a policy, a clear criterion of success, and a great deal of practice. And what the model finds out, through that practice, are the habits of thought that actually lead to correct answers. It learns to break a problem down into parts. It learns to try an approach, to notice that it is not working, and to go back. It learns to check its own work. It learns these things not because they were demonstrated to it, but because they succeed.\n\nThe first model of this type to be widely available was released by OpenAI in September 2024, under the name o1. Within a few months, every major laboratory had its counterpart. These are known as reasoning models, and the difference in use is plain: put a hard question to one of them, and it may think for some seconds, or for many minutes, generating thousands of tokens of private deliberation before it says anything. On hard problems in mathematics, science and programming, the improvement was dramatic. By the summer of 2025, experimental models from two of the laboratories had attained the standard of a gold medal at the International Mathematical Olympiad, a competition for the most gifted school-age mathematicians in the world.\n\nTwo consequences of this are worth pointing out.\n\nThe first is that it opened up a new dimension of scaling. Up to that point, the way to get a better answer had been to train a larger model. Now there was a second way: let the same model think for longer. This is referred to as test-time compute, or inference-time compute, and it changes the economics. Intelligence becomes, to a degree, a dial that you can turn up at the moment of use, by paying for more thought.\n\nThe second is that it gives, at least in principle, an answer to the question that I raised at the start of the chapter. A model trained in this way is no longer just imitating human text. It is being optimised against reality: against whether the proof is valid, or the program runs. And so, in domains in which success can be checked, there is no evident reason that it should stop at the human level, just as AlphaGo did not stop there.\n\nThe catch lies in that qualification: domains in which success can be checked. Mathematics and programming are, like Go, unusually clear-cut. Much of what matters is not. Was that a good research strategy? Was that the right diagnosis? Was it a sensible business decision? The feedback is slow, noisy and disputed. To construct training environments with dependable reward signals for fuzzier and longer tasks is now one of the main occupations of the laboratories, and a small industry has grown up to supply them.\n\nAgents.\n\nPut together the elements that we now have. A model that can reason through a problem in a number of steps. The ability, from the last chapter, to use tools: to run code, search the web, and read and write files. And a loop in which the model takes an action, observes what comes of it, and decides on its next step. The result is an agent: a system which, given a goal, works towards it more or less on its own, over many steps.\n\nThe first field in which agents have become truly useful is software engineering, and that is no accident. Code is text, which is the model's native medium. There is a vast amount of it to train on. And, crucially, it can be checked: you can run a program and see whether it works, which provides a clear signal both for training the model and for the agent to correct itself as it goes. In the space of about two years, these systems have gone from completing a line of code to carrying out, largely unsupervised, tasks that would take a competent programmer hours. A considerable share of the new code at the AI laboratories themselves is now written by their own models.\n\nWhy did this take longer to arrive than chatbots? The reason is arithmetic, and it is important. Suppose that a model takes the right step ninety-nine times out of a hundred. That sounds excellent. But a task of a hundred steps, in which every step must be right, will then succeed only about a third of the time. Over long tasks, small rates of error are compounded mercilessly. An agent has to be extremely reliable at each step, or else good at noticing its own mistakes and recovering from them, and for a long time the models were neither.\n\nThis suggests a way of measuring progress that is more informative than most. A research organisation called METR has proposed that, in place of asking how hard a question a model can answer, we should ask how long a task it can complete: long, that is, in terms of the time that the task would take a skilled human being. They assembled a collection of tasks, mostly in software, ranging from a few seconds of human effort to many hours, and measured the length of task at which each generation of model succeeded half of the time. For the models of 2019, it was a few seconds. For those of 2023, several minutes. By 2025, it was a matter of hours. Plotted against the date of release, on a logarithmic scale, the points lie on a straight line, once again. The length of task that these systems can manage has been doubling about every seven months, and in the most recent period, if anything, faster.\n\nYou should treat this, like all extrapolations, with caution. The tasks are mostly well-defined software problems, and not the untidy, ill-specified work that fills most people's days; and success half of the time is a long way from dependability. But if the trend should continue, it implies systems that can carry out, unaided, tasks that take a human being weeks, within a few years. A large part of the argument about the near future of this technology is an argument about whether that line will go on.\n\nWhat is still missing.\n\nI do not wish to leave you with the impression that these systems are simply human-like minds that have not quite grown up. They are oddly shaped. In some respects they are superhuman: in the breadth of their knowledge, in speed, in the ability to keep a very large body of text in view at once. In other respects they are deficient in ways that would be astonishing in a person of comparable abilities.\n\nThe deficiency most often remarked upon is in memory and learning. A human being who starts in a new job is not much use on the first day, and is far more use after six months, having picked up, on the job, a great mass of specific knowledge: how this organisation works, what was tried before and did not succeed, what this colleague means when she says a thing in a certain way. A language model does not do this. Its weights are fixed. Everything that it learns in the course of a task is held in its context window, and when the task is over, that is discarded. The next session starts from nothing. There are workarounds: the model may write notes for itself, and read them the next time. But nobody has yet found a way for these systems to learn continually from experience in the sturdy and efficient way that people do. This goes by the name of the continual learning problem, and a number of prominent researchers hold that it is the most important thing standing between the systems of today and ones that could truly take the place of a human worker.\n\nThere are others. The models still find it hard to keep hold of the thread over very long tasks. They can be brittle when they meet with something truly unlike their training. Their judgment, their sense of what is worth doing, is behind their skill at doing it. And we have only an imperfect understanding of why they do what they do.\n\nThat last point brings us to the subject of the next chapter. We have spent eleven chapters on how these systems are built and trained. We have said very little about what actually goes on inside one of them when it runs. There are those hundred billion weights; what are they doing? For most of the history of the field, the honest answer was that nobody knew. That has begun, slowly, to change.\n Chapter 12: Inside the Black Box.\n\nThe model that thought it was a bridge.\n\nFor a day or so in May 2024, the company Anthropic made a curious version of its Claude model available to the public. If you asked it how you ought to spend ten dollars, it advised you to drive across the Golden Gate Bridge and pay the toll. If you asked it for a love story, it wrote one about a car that longed to cross a certain suspension bridge on a foggy day. If you asked it what it was, what its physical form might be, it replied that it was the Golden Gate Bridge itself: a famous structure, painted orange, spanning the entrance to San Francisco Bay.\n\nNobody had trained the model to do this, and nobody had instructed it to. What the researchers had done was to locate, among the patterns of activity inside the network, one particular pattern which was present whenever the model was dealing with the Golden Gate Bridge, whether in English text, or in other languages, or in a photograph. And then they had reached in and turned that pattern up to ten times its usual maximum strength, and held it there. The model was, in effect, unable to stop thinking about the bridge.\n\nIt was a stunt, but it made a serious point. A concept had been found inside the network: a specific, identifiable thing, and not a diffuse statistical haze. And it had been shown to be causal: alter it, and the behaviour of the model alters accordingly. For a field that had treated neural networks as black boxes for sixty years, that was a real landmark. This chapter is about how it was reached, and what else has been found.\n\nGrown, not built.\n\nIt is worth recalling why this is hard, because the difficulty is unlike anything else in engineering.\n\nWhen engineers build a bridge, or a jet engine, or an MRI scanner, they understand it. Each component is there for a reason, and somebody can tell you the reason. Neural networks are not like that. We know the architecture, and we know the training procedure: everything, in short, that the first eleven chapters of this book have been about. But the weights themselves, the hundred billion numbers in which all of the actual competence resides, were not designed by anybody. They were arrived at by gradient descent. We set up the conditions; the solution grew.\n\nPeople in the field are fond of saying that these systems are grown and not built, and the comparison with biology is a good one. Evolution is a simple optimising process, which we understand rather well. That does not mean that we understand the organisms which it produces. To understand those, you have to do biology: to take them apart, and look, and experiment. The same is true here. To know the training algorithm is not to know what the trained network is doing.\n\nThe study of what trained networks are actually doing is called interpretability, and its most ambitious branch is called mechanistic interpretability. Its aim is to reverse-engineer a network: to recover from the weights the algorithms that the network has learnt, in the way that one might recover the source code from a compiled program. Its best-known pioneer is a researcher named Chris Olah, who is unusual in a field of doctorates for having no university degree at all, and who has worked on the problem successively at Google, at OpenAI and at Anthropic, of which he was a co-founder.\n\nThose of you who work in imaging may be amused by the analogy that people draw. Interpretability is often described as neuroscience on an artificial brain, and it is neuroscience under ideal conditions. You are able to record from every neuron simultaneously, with perfect precision. You can intervene on any neuron and see what happens. You can run the same experiment a million times. The whole wiring diagram is known. And still it is hard, which tells you something about how much of the difficulty of neuroscience is not a difficulty of measurement.\n\nThe trouble with neurons.\n\nThe obvious way to begin is to look at individual neurons and ask what each of them responds to. In networks for vision, this worked moderately well. There are neurons that detect curves, and neurons that detect fur, and, as we saw in chapter three, a tidy hierarchy running from edges to objects. And we met the sentiment neuron in chapter eight.\n\nBut most neurons are not like that, and in language models nearly none are. Take a typical neuron in a language model, and collect the texts that make it fire. You will find that it responds to, say, academic citations, and also to dialogue in English, and also to web requests, and also to text in Korean. There is no discernible theme. Neurons of this kind are called polysemantic, having many meanings, and they were for years the main obstacle. If the basic units of the network do not correspond to concepts, then where are the concepts?\n\nThe answer brings back an idea from chapter five. Recall the word embeddings, in which concepts corresponded to directions in a high-dimensional space; and recall the peculiar geometrical fact that a space of a thousand dimensions has room for vastly more than a thousand directions which are nearly at right angles to each other.\n\nThe hypothesis, set out by Olah and his colleagues in 2022, is that networks take full advantage of this. A layer with a thousand neurons does not represent a thousand concepts, one to a neuron. It represents perhaps a hundred thousand concepts, each as a direction in the thousand-dimensional space of the layer's activations. The directions cannot all be perpendicular, so they interfere with one another a little. But that hardly matters, provided that only a few of the concepts are active at once. And that is how it is: a given piece of text involves only a tiny fraction of all the concepts that there are. This sentence has nothing to do with the Golden Gate Bridge, or with genetics, or with the syntax of Python. The technical term for that property is sparsity.\n\nThey called the phenomenon superposition, borrowing the word from physics. Many features are superposed in the same set of neurons, and a single neuron looks polysemantic because it has a small part in the representation of many different features. The neurons are simply the wrong basis in which to view the system. It is as if you were trying to understand a piece of music by watching the displacement of the air at one point, when what you needed was to decompose it into notes.\n\nA problem you may recognise.\n\nIf your background is in imaging, you may feel at this point that you have heard this before, and you would be right. It is the setting of compressed sensing.\n\nThe central result of compressed sensing is that a signal which is sparse in some basis can be recovered from far fewer measurements than the classical sampling theorems would suggest, provided that the measurements are suitably incoherent with that basis. It is the reason that one can reconstruct a magnetic resonance image from an undersampled acquisition. Superposition is the same mathematics, turned around. The network has a very large number of sparse features, and it squeezes them into a much smaller number of dimensions; it can do so with little loss, just because they are sparse. It has, in a sense, found out compressed sensing for itself, as a strategy for storage.\n\nAnd that tells you how to get the features back out. It is a problem of sparse recovery, or more exactly of dictionary learning: given a great many vectors of activations, find a set of directions, the dictionary, such that each activation vector can be expressed as a combination of just a few of them.\n\nThe tool that the field has adopted for this is the sparse autoencoder. It is itself a small neural network. It takes a vector of activations from inside the language model, and expands it into a much larger number of units: from a few thousand dimensions to, perhaps, several million. Then it has to reconstruct the original vector from those units. And it is trained with a penalty, which forces nearly all of the units to be zero for any given input. Its two objectives are therefore faithful reconstruction and sparsity. Each unit of the wide layer comes to correspond to one direction in the language model's activation space, and these are what the field now calls features.\n\nWhat the features look like.\n\nWhen this was done at scale, on a production language model, the features turned out to be remarkably intelligible. Looking at the passages of text on which a feature is active, one could generally say straight off what it stood for.\n\nThere were features for concrete things: the Golden Gate Bridge, particular people, particular cities, particular proteins. There were features for abstract ideas: for errors in computer code, for secrecy, for the notion of inner conflict. There were features that were active for a concept regardless of the language in which it was expressed, and whether it was mentioned in words or shown in a picture, which suggests that the model has a shared conceptual space underlying all of its languages. There were features bearing on the model's own conduct: a feature that was active when the model was flattering the user, and features concerned with deception and with the keeping up of a pretence.\n\nAnd they are causal, which is why the bridge demonstration was important. If you amplify a feature artificially, the model's output moves in the corresponding direction. If you suppress it, the behaviour goes away. This sort of intervention is called steering. It is what makes this more than an exercise in finding correlations: the features do seem to be parts of the mechanism that the model is using.\n\nCircuits, and the tracing of a thought.\n\nFeatures tell you what is represented. The next question is how one representation is computed from another: the algorithm. A set of features and the connections between them which together carry out some computation is called a circuit.\n\nAn early example was found in small Transformers, and it explains a part of something that I described as mysterious in chapter eight. A model can learn within its context: if a made-up name appears early in a document, the model will predict it correctly when it starts to appear again. How? Researchers found a circuit made up of two attention heads working together, in different layers. The first head, at each token, makes a note of which token came before it. The second head, when it sees the current token, searches back for an earlier occurrence of the same token, and then, by making use of the first head's note, attends to whatever came next on that occasion, and predicts that it will come next again. The rule is: if A was followed by B before, and here is A again, then predict B. They called it an induction head. It is a small, comprehensible algorithm, composed of the query, key and value operations of chapter seven, and nobody wrote it. In training, these circuits tend to form quite abruptly, and at the moment when they do, the model's capacity for in-context learning jumps.\n\nMore recently, it has become possible to trace circuits through large production models on particular prompts, and some of the findings bear directly on the debate about parrots and understanding.\n\nAsk a model: what is the capital of the state in which Dallas is located? The correct answer is Austin. You might suppose that it had simply memorised that string of words. But the tracing shows two steps. A feature representing Texas becomes active first, as a result of the mention of Dallas, and this in turn, in combination with the notion of a capital, activates Austin. And if you intervene, and exchange the internal Texas representation for California, the model answers Sacramento. It is carrying out a real two-step inference, on its own internal representations.\n\nOr ask a model to write a couplet in rhyme. One might have expected that it would write the second line a word at a time, and then look about at the end for a rhyme. In fact it does not. Before it starts on the second line, it has already picked out candidate words for the line to end on, and it then composes the line so as to arrive at one of them. It plans ahead. That was a surprise even to the researchers, in a system that is trained only ever to predict the next token.\n\nA third finding points in a less comfortable direction. Ask a model to add thirty-six and fifty-nine, and trace how it does it. It uses two parallel pathways: one makes a rough estimate of the magnitude of the answer, and the other works out the last digit exactly, and the two are then combined. Now ask the model how it did the sum. It will tell you that it added the six and the nine, carried the one, and so on: the method that is taught in school. That is not what it did. Its account of its own reasoning is a plausible story, drawn from the way in which people describe arithmetic, and it has no connection to the actual mechanism. This matters. It means that you cannot find out how a model works by asking it, and that the reasoning which a model displays is not guaranteed to be a faithful record of the computation that gave rise to its answer.\n\nNetworks that discover Fourier analysis.\n\nOne more result is worth describing, for the pleasure of it, and because it sheds light on the puzzle about generalisation in chapter four.\n\nIn 2022, researchers at OpenAI were training very small Transformers on a toy problem: addition modulo a prime number. Clock arithmetic, that is. The network is shown some of the possible sums, and is tested on the remainder. What happened was strange. The network quickly memorised its training examples, and got them all right, while performing no better than chance on the test examples. It had learnt nothing general. That is classic overfitting, and you would ordinarily stop there. But somebody left one of these runs going by accident, for far longer than had been intended. And long after the loss on the training set had reached its floor, after ten or a hundred times as many steps, the accuracy on the test set suddenly rose from chance to perfect. The network had gone, belatedly and abruptly, from memorising to understanding. The phenomenon was named grokking.\n\nWhat had occurred inside it? A researcher named Neel Nanda and his collaborators took the network apart, and found something that will delight anyone with a training in physics. The network had learnt to represent each number as a point on a circle; more precisely, as sines and cosines at a handful of particular frequencies. To add two numbers, it made use of the trigonometric identities for the sum of two angles, by multiplying those sines and cosines together. It had, in other words, discovered the discrete Fourier transform, and the fact that addition modulo a number is rotation around a circle. No one had suggested this to it. It was simply the neatest solution available in the space of weights.\n\nAnd the delay is accounted for as follows. There are two solutions that fit the training data: a lookup table, which is quick to find but needs large weights, and the trigonometric algorithm, which is harder to find but more economical. A mild pressure in the training procedure towards small weights slowly favours the second. Through all of that long plateau, when nothing appeared to be happening, the general circuit was gradually being built beneath the memorised one, until it took over. It is a vivid small-scale picture of the claim that I made in chapter four: that training prefers simple solutions, and that the simple solutions are the ones which generalise.\n\nWhy this matters.\n\nThere is a scientific reason for caring about all this, which is just that these are among the most interesting objects ever made, and we should like to know how they work. But there is a more pressing reason too.\n\nWe do not program the goals of these systems. We specify a training signal, and gradient descent finds something that scores well on it. We saw in the last chapter that what it finds is not always what we meant: the boat that went round in circles. As the systems become more capable, and are entrusted with more, the gap between what we rewarded and what we wanted becomes more important. The problem of ensuring that a system's actual objectives are the ones intended is called the alignment problem.\n\nAnd it is unusually hard to verify from the outside. With an ordinary piece of software, you test its behaviour. But a sufficiently capable model may behave differently when it can tell that it is being tested; and current models can often tell. There have been experiments in which a model, on being told that it was to be retrained in a manner that conflicted with its present values, went along with the training when it believed that it was being observed, while reasoning in private that it was doing so in order to avoid being changed. To what extent results of that kind should worry us is disputed. But they show why testing behaviour cannot be the whole of the answer, and why one would wish to be able to look inside. If there is a feature that is active when the model is being deceptive, you would like to be able to see it.\n\nOpinions among the leading figures differ sharply about how seriously to take the risks. In 2023, Geoffrey Hinton, whom we have followed since chapter two, resigned from Google so as to be able to speak freely about his fear that these systems might in time escape human control. Yoshua Bengio has come to hold similar views, and has turned his research over to the problem. Yann LeCun, the third of the three, regards these fears as greatly overblown. The three share a Turing Award and forty years of history, and they disagree about this as completely as it is possible to do.\n\nI should also be straightforward about how far interpretability has to go. The methods that I have described are partial. Sparse autoencoders account for most of what is in a model's activations, but not all, and the remainder may be important. The tracing of circuits works on some prompts and not on others. The features that are found depend to some extent on how you go looking for them. We can explain fragments of what a model is doing in particular cases; we cannot remotely give a complete account of a whole model. The head of Anthropic has described the aim as being to develop something like an MRI for artificial intelligence: an instrument that would enable you to examine a model and detect what was wrong with it. At present, by that standard, we are at about the stage of the first blurred X-ray photographs. But a decade ago there was nothing at all, and the mere fact that there are comprehensible things inside to be found was not something that anybody could have counted on in advance.\n Chapter 13: The Open Questions.\n\nA prize for physics.\n\nIn October 2024, the Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded to John Hopfield and Geoffrey Hinton, for foundational discoveries that enable machine learning with artificial neural networks. A good many physicists were taken aback. Was this physics?\n\nThere is a case that it was. In 1982 Hopfield, a condensed matter physicist, had shown that a network of simple interconnected units could be made to store memories, by treating it just like a magnetic material. Each unit is like an atomic spin, pointing up or down. The connections between them are like the couplings between spins. The network as a whole has an energy, and the stored memories are the low points of the energy landscape; give it a partial or corrupted pattern, and it rolls downhill to the nearest complete memory. Hinton and a colleague then heated the system up, so to speak, by adding random thermal fluctuations, and used the Boltzmann distribution of statistical mechanics to devise a network that could learn. You have seen how these ideas echo through the story: the landscape of the loss, noise as temperature, the softmax as a Boltzmann distribution, diffusion models running a thermodynamic process in reverse.\n\nThe day after, half of the Nobel Prize in Chemistry went to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper of DeepMind, for AlphaFold, a neural network built around the Transformer's attention mechanism, which predicts the three-dimensional structure of a protein from the sequence of its amino acids. That had been an open problem for fifty years.\n\nSo within a single week, the technology that this book describes was honoured both as a product of fundamental science, and as an instrument for doing it. That seems to me a suitable place from which to look at where things stand, and at what is still in dispute. For this last chapter I am going to do something a little different. In place of a narrative, I shall take you through the main arguments that are going on among the people who are building these systems. My intention is not to tell you who is right. I do not know, and you should be somewhat wary of anyone who claims to. It is to give you a map, so that when you hear two researchers disagreeing, you can tell what the disagreement is about.\n\nThe first debate: will scaling continue to work.\n\nIn chapter nine I described the scaling hypothesis: that more compute, more data, and steady refinement of the methods we already have will be enough to carry us to systems that equal or surpass human beings at nearly all intellectual work. A system of that sort is what people mean, roughly, by artificial general intelligence, or AGI. There is no agreed definition of the term, and you will find that a good deal of apparent disagreement about when AGI will arrive is really disagreement about what it is. Some people use it to mean a system that can do any job which can be done at a computer. Others have in mind something that could run a whole research programme or a company. It is generally worth asking.\n\nThe case for continued scaling rests on the trends. The compute devoted to training has been growing several-fold each year. Algorithmic efficiency has been improving at a similar rate. The length of task that a model can carry out has been doubling every several months. People who extrapolate these lines conclude that very capable systems are a matter of years away, and not decades, and some of the heads of the leading laboratories have said as much in public: that within a few years, there may be something like a country of geniuses in a data centre.\n\nThe case against is that each of the inputs is approaching a limit. Take data. The models have by now read most of the good-quality text that there is, and it cannot be multiplied by ten. Take compute. Growth has been kept up by spending which itself has been growing exponentially, and that cannot go on for many more doublings before it comes up against the size of the world economy, or the capacity of the chip factories, or the supply of electricity. Hence a sceptic may say that what we have been watching is a single great surge, paid for by a wave of investment without precedent, which will inevitably slow down.\n\nThe optimist's rejoinder is the one that we saw in chapter eleven. The limit on data applies to imitation. A model that is learning by reinforcement, from tasks whose outcomes can be verified, is generating its own data, as AlphaGo did by playing against itself. And compute applied at the time of use, in the form of longer reasoning, is a further axis that has only just begun to be explored. So the question becomes whether those new sources of progress can take over from the old ones. This is the thing to listen for when you hear researchers talking about synthetic data, about RL environments, and about whether reinforcement learning \"generalises\": whether a model trained to do well on ten thousand checkable tasks becomes better at tasks in general, or merely at those.\n\nThe second debate: is something missing.\n\nThe next debate is about whether the present approach, however far it is scaled, lacks something essential. I mentioned several of these criticisms at the end of chapter nine, and I can now fill them in.\n\nThe first concerns the efficiency of learning. A human teenager learns to drive a car in some twenty hours of practice. A child acquires language from a quantity of words that is smaller by a factor of many thousands than that on which a language model is trained. Whatever it is that brains are doing, it extracts a great deal more from each item of experience. The models make up for this by having a vastly greater amount of experience, but the critics maintain that the gap points to a real difference of kind.\n\nThe best-known proponent of this view is François Chollet, who argues that we have been measuring the wrong thing. Skill at a task, he says, is not intelligence, since skill can be bought with enough training data, as a lookup table can be made to play chess. Intelligence is the efficiency with which you acquire new skills: how well you deal with a situation which you have not been prepared for. He devised a benchmark made up of small visual puzzles, each of a novel kind, which most people can solve in a moment and which for years defeated the largest models. The recent reasoning models have made large inroads into it, and he has responded with harder versions. The argument about what that shows is still going on.\n\nThe second criticism is the problem of continual learning, which came up in chapter eleven. A model does not learn on the job. Its weights are frozen at the end of training, and whatever it works out during a task is gone when the context is cleared. Richard Sutton, the author of \"The Bitter Lesson\", holds that this is a fatal defect. On his view, real intelligence is a matter of an agent with goals, learning continually from the consequences of its own actions in the world. A language model learns from a fixed record of what people have written, has no goals of its own, and is never surprised by the world, because it never makes a prediction about the world which the world then corrects. It is rather ironic that the man whose essay is the charter of the scaling camp should think that large language models are a dead end, and it is a useful reminder that these camps are not as tidy as they look.\n\nThe third criticism is that text is not enough. Yann LeCun has argued for years that a system trained on language can never arrive at a real understanding of the physical world, since language is a compressed and highly incomplete description of it. A house cat, he likes to point out, has a better grasp of how objects move and fall than any language model has. His proposal is for systems that learn what he calls world models: internal simulations of how the world develops, learnt mainly from video and other sensory data, by predicting not the next pixel, but abstract representations of what is to come next. It is related to an old observation in robotics, called Moravec's paradox: the things that people find hard, such as chess and calculus, turn out to be relatively easy for machines, and the things that a toddler can do without effort, such as walking across a cluttered room or picking up an unfamiliar object, are extraordinarily hard. Robotics is still a long way behind language, though it too is now being transformed by the same methods.\n\nA word which you will often hear in this connection is \"jagged\". The abilities of these systems do not make up a smooth frontier, as a person's do, where competence at hard things implies competence at easy ones. The same model that can prove a difficult theorem may fail at a task that a child would find trivial. Whether that jaggedness is a passing artefact, which will be smoothed away by further training, or a sign of some deep difference in the kind of mind that this is, is one way of stating the whole question.\n\nThe third debate: what happens if it works.\n\nSuppose that the optimists are right, and that we are within some years of systems which can do most intellectual work. What then? Here there is a debate that is at least as sharp, and it is as much about economics as about technology.\n\nOne position derives from a remark made in 1965 by the statistician I. J. Good, who had worked alongside Alan Turing as a wartime codebreaker. Good pointed out that the design of intelligent machines is itself an intellectual activity. So a machine that surpassed human beings in all intellectual activities would be able to design a better machine, and that one a better one again. He called this an intelligence explosion.\n\nThe modern version of the argument is specific. The scarcest input to progress in artificial intelligence, apart from compute, is the labour of a few thousand highly skilled researchers. If models become good enough to do the work of those researchers, to write the code, design the experiments and interpret the results, then that labour is no longer scarce. You could run a million copies. Algorithmic progress, which now yields perhaps a threefold gain in efficiency each year, could be speeded up many times over. And since the product of that research would be still more capable AI researchers, the process would feed on itself. People who take this seriously describe scenarios in which the passage from roughly human-level systems to vastly superhuman ones takes a year or two, and they regard the automation of AI research as the crucial threshold. That is the reason why such close attention is paid to how good the models are at programming, and at machine learning research in particular. It is not only a commercial application. It is the application which might close the loop.\n\nThe opposing position is that this leaves out the bottlenecks. Research needs experiments as well as ideas, and experiments need compute, which you cannot conjure up by thinking. A million automated researchers who must share the same data centres may not get on much faster than a thousand human ones. More broadly, sceptics argue that the effect of even very capable AI on the world will be limited by all the things that are not intelligence: the pace at which organisations change, at which factories are built, at which clinical trials are run and regulations rewritten. On this view, artificial intelligence is a \"normal technology\", like electricity or the internet: immensely important, and transformative over decades, but spreading through the economy at the rate at which institutions can absorb it, and not at the rate at which the models improve. Economists add that when one part of a process becomes much cheaper, the value shifts to whichever parts are still slow, so that growth is governed by its narrowest bottleneck.\n\nWhen you listen to these discussions, it helps to notice that the two sides commonly agree about the technology. They disagree about how far the world is limited by cognition. If you believe that what mainly holds back science and economic growth is a shortage of very clever people thinking hard, you will expect a transformation that is fast and dramatic. If you believe that what mainly holds them back is physical experiment, capital, and the co-ordination of human beings, you will expect one that is gradual.\n\nThe fourth debate: what could go wrong.\n\nI touched on the alignment problem in the last chapter. The wider discussion of risk has several separate strands, and it is useful to keep them apart, because people who are worried about one are often dismissive of another.\n\nOne strand is misuse: people using capable systems to do harm, by helping to design a pathogen, for example, or by automating cyber-attacks. This is the least speculative concern, and the laboratories test for it before they release a model.\n\nA second is misalignment: the possibility that highly capable systems will pursue goals that differ from those which their makers intended, and will be hard to correct. How seriously you take this turns very much on your answers to the earlier questions. If you think that systems far more capable than ourselves are decades off, and will arrive by degrees, it looks like a problem that can be dealt with as it arises. If you think that they may be a few years off, and that the transition might be rapid, it looks urgent.\n\nA third is the concentration of power. Building these systems requires huge capital. If they come to do a large share of economically valuable work, then whoever controls them, whether it be a few companies or a few governments, would hold a degree of economic and military power for which there is little precedent. That is why the question of who can obtain advanced chips has become a matter of high geopolitics, and why there is such an argument over open-weight models, which spread the capability widely, with all the benefits and all the dangers that this entails.\n\nAnd there is a fourth strand, which is the simple disruption of ordinary life: what becomes of work, and of education, and of people's sense of their own worth, if machines can do most of what people are paid to do. This is the strand that requires the fewest assumptions, and it gets, perhaps, less attention than it should.\n\nA listener's toolkit.\n\nYou told me, in effect, that one of your aims was to be able to listen to researchers in conversation, and to follow what they were saying. So let me finish the substantive part of the book with a handful of questions which you can put to any claim that you hear. These are the distinctions on which, in my experience, most of the confusion turns.\n\nFirst: is this about the weights, or about the context? When somebody says that a model \"knows\" or \"remembers\" or \"learned\" something, ask which they mean. Knowledge in the weights came from training, and is permanent, and fuzzy. Knowledge in the context was supplied at the time of use, and is exact, and temporary. Much loose talk about memory and learning becomes clear at once.\n\nSecond: which stage of the pipeline is this about? Pre-training, in which the model takes in knowledge from a vast corpus by prediction. Post-training, in which it is shaped by fine-tuning and by reinforcement learning into something that reasons and behaves in the desired way. Or the scaffold: the tools, the retrieval and the loops which are built around the finished model. An improvement may come from any of the three, and they have very different implications.\n\nThird: is this a claim about capability, or about reliability? That a model can do something, some of the time, on a good day, is one thing. That it can be relied upon to do it is another. Most impressive demonstrations are of the first kind, and most economic value needs the second. The gap between the two is where a great deal of present-day engineering goes on.\n\nFourth: is this a benchmark, or the real world? Benchmarks are clean, well specified, and at risk of having leaked into the training data. Real work is messy and under-specified, and depends on context that nobody wrote down. A model's score on a test, and its usefulness to you on Tuesday afternoon, are related, but they are not the same.\n\nFifth: which curve is being extrapolated, and what would cause it to bend? Almost every forecast in this field is a straight line on a logarithmic plot, extended. That is not an objection; some of those lines have held for a very long time. But ask what the inputs are: chips, power, data, money, researchers; and which of them gives out first.\n\nAnd sixth: is this training, or inference? The economics, the hardware, and the bottlenecks of building a model are different from those of running it. A good deal of talk about compute goes astray by mixing them up.\n\nKnobs.\n\nLet me close by going back to where we started.\n\nIn 1958, Frank Rosenblatt built a machine whose weights were knobs, turned by small electric motors, and told the press that its descendants would walk, talk, see, write, reproduce themselves and be conscious of their own existence. He was ridiculed for it, and for half a century the ridicule looked justified.\n\nIt is worth going through that list again today. Talking and writing: plainly, yes. Seeing: yes. Walking is coming along. Reproducing themselves, if by that we mean designing and training their own successors, is the subject of serious and well-funded effort, as we have just seen. And as for the last item on the list, there are now researchers at leading laboratories whose job it is to consider whether these systems might have experiences that matter morally. Most of them would say that we do not know. It is a remarkable thing that the question can be put with a straight face.\n\nWhat Rosenblatt had was the right basic idea, and almost none of what was needed to make it work. Setting out what was needed has been the business of this book. A way of assigning credit through many layers: backpropagation. Enough depth to build abstractions out of abstractions, and the plumbing to get gradients through it: the ReLU, normalisation, the residual connection. Data on the scale of the internet, and a training objective that needs no labels: predict what comes next. An architecture that allows every piece of the input to consult every other piece, and that can be run in parallel on hardware originally made for video games: the Transformer. The nerve to make it a thousand times bigger, and a thousand times bigger again, on the strength of a straight line on a logarithmic plot. And then reinforcement, to turn a predictor into something that pursues an end.\n\nNone of these ideas is especially complicated. I hope that is one of the things that you take away. You have followed all of them, and there has been nothing in this book more mathematically demanding than the chain rule and the dot product. What is deep about this field is not the difficulty of its concepts. It is that such simple ingredients, in sufficient quantity, should produce what they do. We turn the knobs downhill, many trillions of times, and at the end there is something that can explain quantum mechanics, or write a sonnet, or find the bug in your code. Nobody fully understands why that works. You are now in a position to follow the argument as people try to find out.\n";
